data: {"seq":145,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:16:49.579Z","text":"sub_4065B4 is the DHCPv6 packet receive handler — standard dhcp6 server logic. It processes DHCPv6 Solicit/Request/Relay-Forward. The sink sub_40F0F8 is not called here (no callee list has it). The P0 AI path said get_duid → sub_40F0F8, but actually get_duid calls sub_40F168 (which calls sub_40F0F8 with a fixed format \"echo \\\"====>>>>%s\\\" > /dev/ttyS0 \\r\\n\"). Interfaces args (argv) not network. The debug echo writes serial console. Not attacker controlled.\n\nOK so dhcp6s's sub_40F0F8 chain appears non-exploitable from network (only debug echo of fixed strings with argv-derived interface name — but get_duid passes aEth02 constant to sub_40F168, not a3). So the P0 path is likely a false positive for network, but vsprintf to 260-byte stack in sub_40F168 with a3 (interface name from argv) is at most argv-controlled.\n\nRather than beating dhcp6s sys_run_command further, let me look at dhcp6c's sys_run_command (sub_404884) and sys_run_command_2 (sub_40D068) — dhcp6c processes control commands from network (dhcp6_ctl_acceptcommand with sub_404884 as dispatcher, per AS-003). Also dhcp6c's control channel: dhcp6_ctl_init? For dhcp6c, ctlport default 5470? Let me check how sub_404884 parses commands — the \"control message\" protocol: type=0 (command) with base64 payload etc.\n\nAlso let me shift to other high-value targets: \n1. httpd (9582aa23) — 4882 functions. Command injection in TP-Link httpd is well-known (e.g., /cgi or userRpm with backticks). Let me look at httpd's system() sinks with sprintf.\n2. minidlnad (3e3258c9) — SendResp handlers, maybe format string or buffer overflow; also it has system() sinks sys_run_command @ 0x405594, 0x405d5c, 0x416168.\n\nStrategy per user: 动静结合, run dynamic early. Let me request a trace for dhcp6s to see if it even runs (port 547 UDP; http GET won't trigger, but baseline functions count). Actually the trace tool sends an HTTP GET to a port — for dhcp6s control port TCP? dhcp6_ctl_init uses... let me check quickly. In kame dhcp6ctl: UDP port 5470 by default. Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe I should first check what the fuzz tool supports. Fuzz is function-level with args. For sub_40F0F8, function = 0x40f0f8, args [format, arg]? Handshake may fail; fall back to whole binary.\n\nLet me prioritize concretely:\n\nA) dhcp6c control channel command injection (AS-003 dispatchers: sub_404884 sys_run_command, sub_40D068 sys_run_command_2). The control protocol accepts commands over network (UDP/TCP 5470?). Let me read dhcp6c main + dhcp6_ctl_acceptcommand + sub_404884.\n\nB) httpd command injection — very common on TP-Link. Let me search httpd sinks: sprintf+system. Patterns in this httpd: it's the \"httpd\" from TP-Link with /userRpm/, uses function pointers based on URL. Commands executed via system() in functions like \"wm_dyn","stream":true,"block":0}

