data: {"seq":170,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:16:58.365Z","text":"Very interesting find in dhcp6c's sub_404884 (client6_recvreply):\n\n```c\nsystem(\"echo 111 >> /tmp/debugInfo\");\nv15 = dhcp6_stcodestr(*((_WORD *)v12 + 6));\nsprintf(s, \"echo %s %s >> /tmp/debugInfo\", \"client6_recvreply\", v15);\nsystem(s);\n```\n\n`s` is 128 bytes. v15 = dhcp6_stcodestr(status_code) — returns a fixed string from a table (status code → string), NOT attacker controlled directly. dhcp6_stcodestr maps status codes to fixed strings like \"Success\", \"UnspecFail\", etc. So v15 is not directly attacker-controlled. Hmm. But wait, is dhcp6_stcodestr a table lookup with fallback? Let me check dhcp6_stcodestr. If it returns a pointer into a fixed table, then sprintf(s, \"echo %s %s ...\", fixed, v15) is safe.\n\nThe bigger fish: `client6_script(*(char **)(ifconfbyid + 68), v100, v101)` — executes a script path; ifconfig path from config (not network).\n\nActually, let me look at setStatus, setDns — those take network-derived data (status word, nameserver address string). setDns(in6addr2str(...)) — hmm.\n\nLet me look at dhcp6_stcodestr to check.\n\nBut wait — my prime directive: run dynamic verification early. The trace system only sends HTTP GET. dhcp6c/dhcp6s are UDP services. minidlnad and httpd are TCP/HTTP — those are traceable!\n\nLet me re-check the trace status for minidlnad: previous traces with argv [\"-d\",\"-i\",\"lo\"] → ConnectionRefused on port 8200. Note earlier trace \"failed\" with \"unsafe library search path in ELF: '/home/pc/.../minidlna/lib'\" — the minidlnad ELF has RPATH which qemu refused. But later traces with same binary (3e3258c9) got status ok with 47 baseline functions. So qemu can run it now. But connection refused at port 8200. minidlnad default port is 8200 with -p option; the config file minidlna.conf sets port. Without config, default 8200. But it needs DLNA media dirs etc. Connection refused suggests it didn't bind — maybe because it daemonized or crashed.\n\nWait, one trace had argv [\"-d\",\"-i\",\"lo\"] and got 47 functions; another with argv [] got 40. Both refused connections. The firma trace harness runs the binary in qemu-user with a timeout then sends HTTP GET. qemu-user doesn't support threads/networking the way a real process does — minidlnad uses pthreads; qemu-user emulates single process, networking works via host syscalls. It should bind. Possibly it needs config","stream":true,"block":0}

