ait, one trace had argv [\"-d\",\"-i\",\"lo\"] and got 47 functions; another with argv [] got 40. Both refused connections. The firma trace harness runs the binary in qemu-user with a timeout then sends HTTP GET. qemu-user doesn't support threads/networking the way a real process does — minidlnad uses pthreads; qemu-user emulates single process, networking works via host syscalls. It should bind. Possibly it needs config file /etc/minidlna.conf present in the emulated FS. Let me not dig too deep; instead try to run httpd which is the biggest target.\n\nhttpd: previous traces: binary 9582aa23 with argv [] port 80 → \"ConnectionResetError: [Errno 104] Connection reset by peer\". This means httpd DID accept the connection (reset = the connection got accepted then closed). qemu-user + httpd: TP-Link httpd listens on port 80, accepts, reads request. The trigger reset might be because a valid request needs \"Authorization\" or the daemon forked (fork support in qemu-user?) Actually qemu-user supports fork via host fork (single-threaded). TP-Link httpd is single-process with select loop, handles connections.\n\nHTTP GET \"/\" to TP-Link httpd should return something. The reset might happen because httpd expects to read the request before responding, but the harness sends the request... Actually HTTP GET /userRpm/LoginRpm.htm got reset too. Maybe the harness's HTTP client hits a closed socket because httpd's select loop hasn't accepted yet, or the connection is handled then closed without response under qemu (missing /web/ files? no, LoginRpm.htm request processing prints error page).\n\nLet me instead look at httpd to find concrete","stream":true,"block":0}

