data: {"seq":238,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:17:20.731Z","text":"start_pppd in xl2tpd uses execv(\"/usr/sbin/pppd\", argv) — NOT system(). The args are built from tunnel/call attributes. If the call attributes (e.g., username/password: byte_4302B8, byte_4303B8 globals) are set","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":239,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:17:21.089Z","text":"start_pppd in xl2tpd uses execv(\"/usr/sbin/pppd\", argv) — NOT system(). The args are built from tunnel/call attributes. If the call attributes (e.g., username/password: byte_4302B8, byte_4303B8 globals) are set from L2TP control messages (AVPs), then pppd argv can be controlled, but execv is not command injection (no shell). However, pppd has options parsing; injecting argv like \"-k\" or \"pty\" could be dangerous but that's not simple cmdi. The classic","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":240,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:17:21.470Z","text":"start_pppd in xl2tpd uses execv(\"/usr/sbin/pppd\", argv) — NOT system(). The args are built from tunnel/call attributes. If the call attributes (e.g., username/password: byte_4302B8, byte_4303B8 globals) are set from L2TP control messages (AVPs), then pppd argv can be controlled, but execv is not command injection (no shell). However, pppd has options parsing; injecting argv like \"-k\" or \"pty\" could be dangerous but that's not simple cmdi. The classic xl2tpd vuln is with system() in other code paths. This is TP-Link's version of xl2tpd that uses execv. Less clear-cut. Also, note byte_4302B8 etc. are global — set from config file probably (l2tp config), not network. Not the network path.\n\nAlso min","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":241,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:17:21.822Z","text":"start_pppd in xl2tpd uses execv(\"/usr/sbin/pppd\", argv) — NOT system(). The args are built from tunnel/call attributes. If the call attributes (e.g., username/password: byte_4302B8, byte_4303B8 globals) are set from L2TP control messages (AVPs), then pppd argv can be controlled, but execv is not command injection (no shell). However, pppd has options parsing; injecting argv like \"-k\" or \"pty\" could be dangerous but that's not simple cmdi. The classic xl2tpd vuln is with system() in other code paths. This is TP-Link's version of xl2tpd that uses execv. Less clear-cut. Also, note byte_4302B8 etc. are global — set from config file probably (l2tp config), not network. Not the network path.\n\nAlso minidlna \"ff_*\" paths are from libavformat.so (a shared library), not an entry binary — those paths are inside the shared library; reachability requires minidlnad to call them. minidlnad uses libavformat for transcoding. The strcpy in ff_rtsp_connect is only triggered when the router","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":242,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:17:22.438Z","text":"start_pppd in xl2tpd uses execv(\"/usr/sbin/pppd\", argv) — NOT system(). The args are built from tunnel/call attributes. If the call attributes (e.g., username/password: byte_4302B8, byte_4303B8 globals) are set from L2TP control messages (AVPs), then pppd argv can be controlled, but execv is not command injection (no shell). However, pppd has options parsing; injecting argv like \"-k\" or \"pty\" could be dangerous but that's not simple cmdi. The classic xl2tpd vuln is with system() in other code paths. This is TP-Link's version of xl2tpd that uses execv. Less clear-cut. Also, note byte_4302B8 etc. are global — set from config file probably (l2tp config), not network. Not the network path.\n\nAlso minidlna \"ff_*\" paths are from libavformat.so (a shared library), not an entry binary — those paths are inside the shared library; reachability requires minidlnad to call them. minidlnad uses libavformat for transcoding. The strcpy in ff_rtsp_connect is only triggered when the router streams","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":243,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:17:23.000Z","text":"start_pppd in xl2tpd uses execv(\"/usr/sbin/pppd\", argv) — NOT system(). The args are built from tunnel/call attributes. If the call attributes (e.g., username/password: byte_4302B8, byte_4303B8 globals) are set from L2TP control messages (AVPs), then pppd argv can be controlled, but execv is not command injection (no shell). However, pppd has options parsing; injecting argv like \"-k\" or \"pty\" could be dangerous but that's not simple cmdi. The classic xl2tpd vuln is with system() in other code paths. This is TP-Link's version of xl2tpd that uses execv. Less clear-cut. Also, note byte_4302B8 etc. are global — set from config file probably (l2tp config), not network. Not the network path.\n\nAlso minidlna \"ff_*\" paths are from libavformat.so (a shared library), not an entry binary — those paths are inside the shared library; reachability requires minidlnad to call them. minidlnad uses libavformat for transcoding. The strcpy in ff_rtsp_connect is only triggered when the router streams from an RTSP URL (config-driven), not attacker-reachable via DLNA HTTP.\n\nOK. Let me refocus on the three named targets: httpd, minidlna, dhcp6s. And start dynamic ASAP.\n\nDynamic options: fw_request_trace only sends HTTP GET to a port. For httpd: port 80. Previous attempts got \"Connection reset by peer\" — meaning httpd accepted and closed","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":244,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:17:23.354Z","text":"start_pppd in xl2tpd uses execv(\"/usr/sbin/pppd\", argv) — NOT system(). The args are built from tunnel/call attributes. If the call attributes (e.g., username/password: byte_4302B8, byte_4303B8 globals) are set from L2TP control messages (AVPs), then pppd argv can be controlled, but execv is not command injection (no shell). However, pppd has options parsing; injecting argv like \"-k\" or \"pty\" could be dangerous but that's not simple cmdi. The classic xl2tpd vuln is with system() in other code paths. This is TP-Link's version of xl2tpd that uses execv. Less clear-cut. Also, note byte_4302B8 etc. are global — set from config file probably (l2tp config), not network. Not the network path.\n\nAlso minidlna \"ff_*\" paths are from libavformat.so (a shared library), not an entry binary — those paths are inside the shared library; reachability requires minidlnad to call them. minidlnad uses libavformat for transcoding. The strcpy in ff_rtsp_connect is only triggered when the router streams from an RTSP URL (config-driven), not attacker-reachable via DLNA HTTP.\n\nOK. Let me refocus on the three named targets: httpd, minidlna, dhcp6s. And start dynamic ASAP.\n\nDynamic options: fw_request_trace only sends HTTP GET to a port. For httpd: port 80. Previous attempts got \"Connection reset by peer\" — meaning httpd accepted and closed, possibly because request handling requires the full request or auth. Let me try again with a proper URL. Actually the harness sends a GET to port; maybe httpd under qemu actually works but the specific httpd build requires the config file /etc/http... Let me look at how httpd starts: args? The ident","stream":true,"block":0}

