es support threads via clone). The connection reset could be because httpd's accept loop runs in a thread that qemu can't schedule properly, or the select loop rejected the connection because the request wasn't a valid \"GET /... HTTP/1.1\" with Host? The harness sends proper GET though.\n\nLet me look at whether there are prior failed traces with httpd \"ConnectionResetError\" — that means TCP connect succeeded and peer closed. So httpd IS listening and accepting. Good sign. Maybe it needs a specific path that exists in /web. The request \"/\" triggered reset too. Hmm, if httpd requires Authorization header in response to 401... it would send 401 then close. \"Connection reset\" though means RST, not normal close. Maybe the process crashed on the connection (RST when process closes abruptly). Interesting — could itself be a crash. But \"empty diff\" and 30-55 baseline functions.\n\nTo make httpd traces meaningful, we should compare two different requests (baseline vs trigger) — the tool does baseline run vs trigger run. The trigger error means zero diff; the trigger didn't reach the handler. Need httpd to actually respond. Options:\n1. Provide argv that makes httpd run in foreground with a spec. e.g. httpd -p 80? or -f? TP-Link httpd usage: httpd [-p port] [-c config]. Let me check httpd main.\n\nLet me read httpd main (0x...). httpd md5 9582aa23. Search \"main\" → get the address. Actually from the earlier brief, binary httpd has 4882 functions. main address? Let me search.\n\nAlso for minidlna: trace with argv -d -i lo got 47 funcs, refused connection. minidlna default: reads /etc/minidlna.conf, binds 8200. With -d (debug, no daemon) and -i lo... maybe it needs `-f` (foreground). Actually -d is \"daemonize\"? In minidlna: -d = debug (no daemon, log to stderr)? Options: -f foreground, -d debug... Let me check minidlna man: -d = debug, stays in foreground? minidlna options: -f (foreground), -d (debug output)... The trace harness may kill before binding. 47 functions baseline means it ran some code. Connection refused at 8200 means not listening. Maybe it did listen but on the wrong port or on UDP 1900 only. Let me check minidlnad config default port: 8200.\n\nLet me try minidlna trace again with -f -d and port 8200, and also try to run the SSDP UDP? The harness only does","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":253,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:17:26.704Z","text":"start_pppd in xl2tpd uses execv(\"/usr/sbin/pppd\", argv) — NOT system(). The args are built from tunnel/call attributes. If the call attributes (e.g., username/password: byte_4302B8, byte_4303B8 globals) are set from L2TP control messages (AVPs), then pppd argv can be controlled, but execv is not command injection (no shell). However, pppd has options parsing; injecting argv like \"-k\" or \"pty\" could be dangerous but that's not simple cmdi. The classic xl2tpd vuln is with system() in other code paths. This is TP-Link's version of xl2tpd that uses execv. Less clear-cut. Also, note byte_4302B8 etc. are global — set from config file probably (l2tp config), not network. Not the network path.\n\nAlso minidlna \"ff_*\" paths are from libavformat.so (a shared library), not an entry binary — those paths are inside the shared library; reachability requires minidlnad to call them. minidlnad uses libavformat for transcoding. The strcpy in ff_rtsp_connect is only triggered when the router streams from an RTSP URL (config-driven), not attacker-reachable via DLNA HTTP.\n\nOK. Let me refocus on the three named targets: httpd, minidlna, dhcp6s. And start dynamic ASAP.\n\nDynamic options: fw_request_trace only sends HTTP GET to a port. For httpd: port 80. Previous attempts got \"Connection reset by peer\" — meaning httpd accepted and closed, possibly because request handling requires the full request or auth. Let me try again with a proper URL. Actually the harness sends a GET to port; maybe httpd under qemu actually works but the specific httpd build requires the config file /etc/http... Let me look at how httpd starts: args? The ident says /usr/bin/httpd port 80. Under qemu-user, httpd may bind 0.0.0.0:80. The reset suggests it accepted and closed the connection. Many TP-Link httpd forks? Actually the Archer C7 httpd is single process with many threads (pthreads). qemu-user supports threads these days (both linux-user does support threads via clone). The connection reset could be because httpd's accept loop runs in a thread that qemu can't schedule properly, or the select loop rejected the connection because the request wasn't a valid \"GET /... HTTP/1.1\" with Host? The harness sends proper GET though.\n\nLet me look at whether there are prior failed traces with httpd \"ConnectionResetError\" — that means TCP connect succeeded and peer closed. So httpd IS listening and accepting. Good sign. Maybe it needs a specific path that exists in /web. The request \"/\" triggered reset too. Hmm, if httpd requires Authorization header in response to 401... it would send 401 then close. \"Connection reset\" though means RST, not normal close. Maybe the process crashed on the connection (RST when process closes abruptly). Interesting — could itself be a crash. But \"empty diff\" and 30-55 baseline functions.\n\nTo make httpd traces meaningful, we should compare two different requests (baseline vs trigger) — the tool does baseline run vs trigger run. The trigger error means zero diff; the trigger didn't reach the handler. Need httpd to actually respond. Options:\n1. Provide argv that makes httpd run in foreground with a spec. e.g. httpd -p 80? or -f? TP-Link httpd usage: httpd [-p port] [-c config]. Let me check httpd main.\n\nLet me read httpd main (0x...). httpd md5 9582aa23. Search \"main\" → get the address. Actually from the earlier brief, binary httpd has 4882 functions. main address? Let me search.\n\nAlso for minidlna: trace with argv -d -i lo got 47 funcs, refused connection. minidlna default: reads /etc/minidlna.conf, binds 8200. With -d (debug, no daemon) and -i lo... maybe it needs `-f` (foreground). Actually -d is \"daemonize\"? In minidlna: -d = debug (no daemon, log to stderr)? Options: -f foreground, -d debug... Let me check minidlna man: -d = debug, stays in foreground? minidlna options: -f (foreground), -d (debug output)... The trace harness may kill before binding. 47 functions baseline means it ran some code. Connection refused at 8200 means not listening. Maybe it did listen but on the wrong port or on UDP 1900 only. Let me check minidlnad config default port: 8200.\n\nLet me try minidlna trace again with -f -d and port 8200, and also try to run the SSDP UDP? The harness only does HTTP GET.\n\nAlso dhcp6s: UDP only; the trace HTTP GET on UDP port won't work, but the baseline trace at least counts functions. Real verification for dhcp6s would need","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":254,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:17:27.081Z","text":"start_pppd in xl2tpd uses execv(\"/usr/sbin/pppd\", argv) — NOT system(). The args are built from tunnel/call attributes. If the call attributes (e.g., username/password: byte_4302B8, byte_4303B8 globals) are set from L2TP control messages (AVPs), then pppd argv can be controlled, but execv is not command injection (no shell). However, pppd has options parsing; injecting argv like \"-k\" or \"pty\" could be dangerous but that's not simple cmdi. The classic xl2tpd vuln is with system() in other code paths. This is TP-Link's version of xl2tpd that uses execv. Less clear-cut. Also, note byte_4302B8 etc. are global — set from config file probably (l2tp config), not network. Not the network path.\n\nAlso minidlna \"ff_*\" paths are from libavformat.so (a shared library), not an entry binary — those paths are inside the shared library; reachability requires minidlnad to call them. minidlnad uses libavformat for transcoding. The strcpy in ff_rtsp_connect is only triggered when the router streams from an RTSP URL (config-driven), not attacker-reachable via DLNA HTTP.\n\nOK. Let me refocus on the three named targets: httpd, minidlna, dhcp6s. And start dynamic ASAP.\n\nDynamic options: fw_request_trace only sends HTTP GET to a port. For httpd: port 80. Previous attempts got \"Connection reset by peer\" — meaning httpd accepted and closed, possibly because request handling requires the full request or auth. Let me try again with a proper URL. Actually the harness sends a GET to port; maybe httpd under qemu actually works but the specific httpd build requires the config file /etc/http... Let me look at how httpd starts: args? The ident says /usr/bin/httpd port 80. Under qemu-user, httpd may bind 0.0.0.0:80. The reset suggests it accepted and closed the connection. Many TP-Link httpd forks? Actually the Archer C7 httpd is single process with many threads (pthreads). qemu-user supports threads these days (both linux-user does support threads via clone). The connection reset could be because httpd's accept loop runs in a thread that qemu can't schedule properly, or the select loop rejected the connection because the request wasn't a valid \"GET /... HTTP/1.1\" with Host? The harness sends proper GET though.\n\nLet me look at whether there are prior failed traces with httpd \"ConnectionResetError\" — that means TCP connect succeeded and peer closed. So httpd IS listening and accepting. Good sign. Maybe it needs a specific path that exists in /web. The request \"/\" triggered reset too. Hmm, if httpd requires Authorization header in response to 401... it would send 401 then close. \"Connection reset\" though means RST, not normal close. Maybe the process crashed on the connection (RST when process closes abruptly). Interesting — could itself be a crash. But \"empty diff\" and 30-55 baseline functions.\n\nTo make httpd traces meaningful, we should compare two different requests (baseline vs trigger) — the tool does baseline run vs trigger run. The trigger error means zero diff; the trigger didn't reach the handler. Need httpd to actually respond. Options:\n1. Provide argv that makes httpd run in foreground with a spec. e.g. httpd -p 80? or -f? TP-Link httpd usage: httpd [-p port] [-c config]. Let me check httpd main.\n\nLet me read httpd main (0x...). httpd md5 9582aa23. Search \"main\" → get the address. Actually from the earlier brief, binary httpd has 4882 functions. main address? Let me search.\n\nAlso for minidlna: trace with argv -d -i lo got 47 funcs, refused connection. minidlna default: reads /etc/minidlna.conf, binds 8200. With -d (debug, no daemon) and -i lo... maybe it needs `-f` (foreground). Actually -d is \"daemonize\"? In minidlna: -d = debug (no daemon, log to stderr)? Options: -f foreground, -d debug... Let me check minidlna man: -d = debug, stays in foreground? minidlna options: -f (foreground), -d (debug output)... The trace harness may kill before binding. 47 functions baseline means it ran some code. Connection refused at 8200 means not listening. Maybe it did listen but on the wrong port or on UDP 1900 only. Let me check minidlnad config default port: 8200.\n\nLet me try minidlna trace again with -f -d and port 8200, and also try to run the SSDP UDP? The harness only does HTTP GET.\n\nAlso dhcp6s: UDP only; the trace HTTP GET on UDP port won't work, but the baseline trace at least counts functions. Real verification for dhcp6s would need fuzz. dhcp6s is not suitable for fuzzing the packet parser via function-level? Let me try fuzz on dhcp6s's dhcp6_get_options or sub_4065B4?","stream":true,"block":0}

