event: thinking
data: {"seq":369,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:18:09.913Z","text":"uclited execWlanCmd builds `iwconfig %s essid %s` etc. with sprintf+system. a2/essid values come from config ops (called from named/status handlers). Who calls execWlanCmd? a2 = interface name, v21 = essid. If the essid comes from an HTTP request via httpd→ipc→uclited, then command injection possible (e.g. essid containing `;`. But TP-Link web UI sanitizes? Typically not for essid. This is the standard TP-Link \"execWlanCmd essid command injection\" but that's normally in uclited via the \"sw\" IPC.\n\nBut wait: how does uclited receive commands? uclited is started by... IN-011 says TCP. Let me check uclited main (0x5009f0) to see the listen setup. Note: uclited likely uses a UNIX domain socket or a TCP 6666. On many TP-Link builds, uclited listens on TCP port 6666 (admind). Let me read main brief.\n\nAlso execFormatCmd @ 0x411658 (vsprintf+sub_41153C(system)). Who calls execFormatCmd with attacker data? Probably USB storage format function (format partition). Let me get callers. Similar to before — fw_call_trace.\n\nActually, let me stop the static sprawl and follow the user's instruction: dynamic verification early. Priority actions:\n1. fw_request_trace for httpd with proper request path to get a non-error trigger. Let me try /userRpm/DiagnosticRpm.htm and /userRpm/PingIframeRpm.htm?doType=ping&ping_addr=127.0.0.1&isNew=new... The harness request_path is a path only (no query). Try \"/userRpm/PingIframeRpm.htm\".\n2. Start fuzz on concrete hypotheses:\n   - minidlna: parser sub_40D1FC is HTTP request dispatch builder (takes a1 = request struct). Function-level fuzz args [a1]? risky. Maybe whole-binary fuzz of minidlnad? \"handshake fail → whole binary\".\n   - httpd: whole-binary fuzz? httpd 4882 functions; whole binary fuzz may be slow.\n\nActually let me check what fuzz targets make sense: fuzz needs a function with args like [buf, len]. For memory corruption: minidlna's parsexml (0x41d8d8), ParseNameValue (0x41c8f8)? These take input buffers from HTTP body (SOAP). parsexml takes (xml buffer, ...). That's a good fuzz target. Let me look at parsexml args.\n\nAlso httpd's httpLineUnescape (0x432fc0) — a normalizer for URL-encoded input. Fuzz [buf,len] could find overflows. Let me check its signature.\n\nLet me get signatures: minidlna parsexml 0x41d8d8 brief, minidlna ParseNameValue 0x41c8f8 brief, httpd httpLineUnescape 0x432fc0 brief, httpd httpBase64decode 0x42faf4 brief.","stream":true,"block":0}

