data: {"seq":391,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:18:20.692Z","text":"parsexml is a struct-based wrapper (a1 is a parser struct), ParseNameValue(a1=name, a2=value, a3=result, a4=?) parses \"name:value\" pairs. These could be fuzz targets with args [buf, len]... ParseNameValue(nvstr, len, result, base). Good fuzz candidate: ParseNameValue with args [\"...Unhandled...:...\", len, ptr, base].\n\nhttpLineUnescape(char*) decodes %XX in place, compressing (strncpy 2 bytes, httpStrCpy shifts) — in-place so no overflow (writes <= reads). Not a good fuzz target.\n\nhttpBase64decode(input, out, outlen) — classic overflow if no length checks. 102 lines with strlen only callee. Let me read its full source to check output bounds handling.\n\nNow let me kick off the dynamic side:\n1. fw_request_trace httpd with /userRpm/PingIframeRpm.htm (the handler sub_48E0F4) — see if we get coverage different from \"/\".  Use argv [] port 80. Since previous httpd trigger = connection reset, diff may still be empty, but it's the mandatory dynamic step.\n\nWait, maybe httpd needs argv like \"-h\"? TP-Link httpd: /usr/bin/httpd [-h hostname]? Let me quickly check httpd main briefly... it's large. Actually the previous trace got 30-55 baseline functions, meaning httpd main ran partially (before the loop?). The trigger resets — httpd accepted then closed; doesn't process. Perhaps because qemu-user lacks proper multiple threads for httpd's worker pool (httpd spawns worker threads; qemu-user can do clone). Or httpd needs /tmp configuration files to exist (e.g., /tmp/module). \n\nLet me not over-invest; do the trace and fuzz submissions and move on.\n\nPlan:\n- Trace 1: httpd, port 80, /userRpm/PingIframeRpm.htm (this hits sub_48E0F4 if reachable).\n- Trace 2: minidlnad, argv [\"-f\",\"-d\",\"-i\",\"lo\"], port 8200, \"/\".\n- Fuzz 1: minidlna ParseNameValue 0x41c8f8 args [text,len,result,base]. Hmm handshake unlikely; but try.\n- Fuzz 2: httpd httpBase64decode 0x42faf4 args [input,out,outlen].\n\nBut fuzz budget max 6; user says only with concrete hypothesis. Let me read httpBase64decode full first to have a hypothesis, then submit fuzz.\n\nActually — let me also check the minidlna sub_40D1FC dangerous memmove: `memmove(dest, src, *(_DWORD *)(a1 + 112))` — that's response building. And sub_450CE0 (dispatcher @ 0x450ce0) parses request line. Let me read sub_450CE0 (the request parsing/dispatch in minidlna: it handles \"GET /path?query HTTP/1.1\"). It's likely the main HTTP parser.\n\nLet me read httpBase64decode + minidlna sub_450CE0 in parallel, then launch dynamic runs.","stream":false,"block":0}

