data: {"seq":475,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:18:58.618Z","text":"Trace: httpd with -I -p 80 → connection refused, baseline 9 functions (only startup, no listen). httpd did not bind with -I? The -I flag might mean \"validate init\". Let me check httpd usage. Actually with no argv before it also refused (reset). Hmm previous trace with argv[] got \"ConnectionReset\" — that means it DID accept. But this run with -I -p 80 refused. So -I is wrong.\n\nFuzz: handshake failed for minidlna ParseNameValue — \"目标函数在正常启动流程中未被到达\". Per instructions: 改整二进制 (只传 binary_md5). minidlna whole-binary fuzz with argv: minidlnad in qemu needs config... Let me try whole-binary mode: fw_request_fuzz binary_md5 only (function omitted) — that runs the whole program with AFL argv/stdin? For a daemon that needs config, might fail too. Let me try with seconds 120 (whole binary).\n\nAlso httpd trace: use argv [] again (worked to get connection reset = accepted). Actually connection reset means accept but close; still no diff. The httpd process under qemu may not process because it forks worker threads; qemu-user does support threads but httpd might use \"wmnet\" select loop in main thread. The reset might be the trigger","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":476,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:18:58.992Z","text":"Trace: httpd with -I -p 80 → connection refused, baseline 9 functions (only startup, no listen). httpd did not bind with -I? The -I flag might mean \"validate init\". Let me check httpd usage. Actually with no argv before it also refused (reset). Hmm previous trace with argv[] got \"ConnectionReset\" — that means it DID accept. But this run with -I -p 80 refused. So -I is wrong.\n\nFuzz: handshake failed for minidlna ParseNameValue — \"目标函数在正常启动流程中未被到达\". Per instructions: 改整二进制 (只传 binary_md5). minidlna whole-binary fuzz with argv: minidlnad in qemu needs config... Let me try whole-binary mode: fw_request_fuzz binary_md5 only (function omitted) — that runs the whole program with AFL argv/stdin? For a daemon that needs config, might fail too. Let me try with seconds 120 (whole binary).\n\nAlso httpd trace: use argv [] again (worked to get connection reset = accepted). Actually connection reset means accept but close; still no diff. The httpd process under qemu may not process because it forks worker threads; qemu-user does support threads but httpd might use \"wmnet\" select loop in main thread. The reset might be the trigger harness hitting the socket before httpd's accept loop starts, or httpd requires the client to send the full request before responding (it waits for more data then responds 401). The harness sends GET and awaits response; httpd closes → reset.\n\nGiven time constraints, focus dynamic budget on fuzz which can find real memory corruption there. Candidates with start-reachable functions: for whole-binary mode the AFL forkserver needs the","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":477,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:18:59.350Z","text":"Trace: httpd with -I -p 80 → connection refused, baseline 9 functions (only startup, no listen). httpd did not bind with -I? The -I flag might mean \"validate init\". Let me check httpd usage. Actually with no argv before it also refused (reset). Hmm previous trace with argv[] got \"ConnectionReset\" — that means it DID accept. But this run with -I -p 80 refused. So -I is wrong.\n\nFuzz: handshake failed for minidlna ParseNameValue — \"目标函数在正常启动流程中未被到达\". Per instructions: 改整二进制 (只传 binary_md5). minidlna whole-binary fuzz with argv: minidlnad in qemu needs config... Let me try whole-binary mode: fw_request_fuzz binary_md5 only (function omitted) — that runs the whole program with AFL argv/stdin? For a daemon that needs config, might fail too. Let me try with seconds 120 (whole binary).\n\nAlso httpd trace: use argv [] again (worked to get connection reset = accepted). Actually connection reset means accept but close; still no diff. The httpd process under qemu may not process because it forks worker threads; qemu-user does support threads but httpd might use \"wmnet\" select loop in main thread. The reset might be the trigger harness hitting the socket before httpd's accept loop starts, or httpd requires the client to send the full request before responding (it waits for more data then responds 401). The harness sends GET and awaits response; httpd closes → reset.\n\nGiven time constraints, focus dynamic budget on fuzz which can find real memory corruption there. Candidates with start-reachable functions: for whole-binary mode the AFL forkserver needs the binary to start and hit a main loop where stdin/args feed AFL. For daemons it's tricky.\n\nAlternative: frida runs. fw_request_frida on an x86 process — but these are mips binaries; frida needs x86 host process. Not","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":478,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:18:59.701Z","text":"Trace: httpd with -I -p 80 → connection refused, baseline 9 functions (only startup, no listen). httpd did not bind with -I? The -I flag might mean \"validate init\". Let me check httpd usage. Actually with no argv before it also refused (reset). Hmm previous trace with argv[] got \"ConnectionReset\" — that means it DID accept. But this run with -I -p 80 refused. So -I is wrong.\n\nFuzz: handshake failed for minidlna ParseNameValue — \"目标函数在正常启动流程中未被到达\". Per instructions: 改整二进制 (只传 binary_md5). minidlna whole-binary fuzz with argv: minidlnad in qemu needs config... Let me try whole-binary mode: fw_request_fuzz binary_md5 only (function omitted) — that runs the whole program with AFL argv/stdin? For a daemon that needs config, might fail too. Let me try with seconds 120 (whole binary).\n\nAlso httpd trace: use argv [] again (worked to get connection reset = accepted). Actually connection reset means accept but close; still no diff. The httpd process under qemu may not process because it forks worker threads; qemu-user does support threads but httpd might use \"wmnet\" select loop in main thread. The reset might be the trigger harness hitting the socket before httpd's accept loop starts, or httpd requires the client to send the full request before responding (it waits for more data then responds 401). The harness sends GET and awaits response; httpd closes → reset.\n\nGiven time constraints, focus dynamic budget on fuzz which can find real memory corruption there. Candidates with start-reachable functions: for whole-binary mode the AFL forkserver needs the binary to start and hit a main loop where stdin/args feed AFL. For daemons it's tricky.\n\nAlternative: frida runs. fw_request_frida on an x86 process — but these are mips binaries; frida needs x86 host process. Not applicable to mips firmware (frida only x86).\n\nOK, let me try whole-binary fuzz for minidlnad with argv to make it run in foreground and read from stdin? minidlnad doesn't read stdin. It's a daemon; the persistent hook needs the target function to be naturally reached during startup. Parsers aren't reached at startup.\n\nBetter: fuzz dhcp6s? Whole binary dhcp6c","stream":true,"block":0}

