data: {"seq":539,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:19:27.834Z","text":"No minidlna.conf in /etc. minidlna with default config path /etc/minidlna.conf missing → maybe exits after options parse fail, but baseline still covered 40-47 functions (some init ran). With -f (foreground) and -d, it should run. Previous trace with -d -i lo had 47 funcs (more than 40 empty). Still connection refused — the process may have exited after failing to find config/media.\n\nminidlna reads config: if no /etc/minidlna.conf, it still runs with defaults (db at /var/cache/minidlna, port 8200, media dir /var/media?). It may fail mkdir. Hmm.\n\nSince minidlna is a DLNA thing, the HTTP server port default 8200 and the config probably allows port via -p. Let me try trace minidlna argv [\"-f\",\"-p\",\"8200\"] port 8200 path \"/\". If connection refused again, minidlnad likely exits under qemu due to missing db dirs.\n\nActually, what does baseline 40-47 funcs cover? Maybe minidlna ran main, read /etc/minidlna.conf (open fails → perror), then exits rc=1. In that case baseline functions = init path only, no listen ever. That's consistent with refusal.\n\nCan I create the config + dirs via the sandbox workspace? fw_request_trace runs inside docker sandbox with the extracted rootfs — I can't modify rootfs through the trace API... The sandbox is a docker container with the rootfs; the tool doesn't expose a way to pre-create files. But workspace is writable; is the workspace mounted into the sandbox? Unknown.\n\nAlternative: use argv to point config: minidlna -c <configfile>. If I can write a config file in a path the sandbox sees... The workspace files may be visible to sandbox? Uncertain.\n\nLet me focus differently. The user wants findings with call chains + PoC. We have limited dynamic capacity. The realistic approach for this round:\n\n1. Continue static hunt for a REAL command injection in httpd (user-visible, unauthenticated-adjacent) — TP-Link C7v2 3.15.4. Known CVE-2018-14335? Actually a widely-advertised Archer C7 vuln in this generation: \"uci execFormatCmd\" — uclited. Let me examine how uclited exposes commands over its IPC and whether there's TCP exposure. Check ucm_msgTaskCreate.\n\n2. dhcp6s/dhcp6c memory bugs: e.g., dhcp6c \"dhcp6_get_options\" parsing with dupid. Known: CVE-2011-... eh.\n\n3. The xl2tpd network_thread strcpy P0 — although xl2tpd excluded from identification, on this firmware xl2tpd is the WAN L2TP client — actually for Archer C7, L2TP WAN client takes control of... it's initiated by the router to an ISP server, so xl2tpd's *inbound* is not expected from LAN/internet. L2TP server mode would be LATM. So network_thread strcpy over UDP 1701 requires the router to have xl2tpd server running — not in this firmware. Skip.\n\nLet me quickly read dhcp6c's dhcp6_get_options → dupid handling and dhcp6_vbuf_copy for memory issues, since dhcp6c IS a listed input (IN-003) — but dhcp6c is a client that READS network replies from the DHCPv6 server... an attacker on the WAN could act as rogue DHCPv6 server → memory corruption in client. That's attacker-reachable (network). Similarly dhcp6s server parses queries from clients on LAN.\n\nFocus: dhcp6s sub","stream":true,"block":0}

