data: {"seq":564,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:19:38.580Z","text":"dhcp6_get_options @ 0x40dc1c in dhcp6s: 531 lines, network input (a1 = options buffer, a2 = end pointer). This is THE packet parser for DHCPv6 messages. A perfect fuzz target: function-level fuzz with args [buf,len]. It's called from sub_4065B4 (recv path) which is the natural caller, but in whole-binary startup it's not reached... For function-level fuzz, AFL-qemu needs the function reached in normal startup OR the harness can call it directly? The error said \"目标函数在正常启动流程中未被到达\" — function-level requires the function naturally reached during startup (persistent mode). dhcp6_get_options is NOT reached at startup. So function-level fuzz will fail again.\n\nHmm, but wait — maybe the fuzz harness works differently: it persists at the function entry when the binary naturally executes it. For startup-reachable functions only. So for parsers that only run on network input, function-level fuzz fails handshake. Unless... the earlier fz-6b17d0a9 failed because minidlna couldn't even start (exits before reaching main loop). dhcp6s main() does reach a loop: it would run sub_4065B4 only on recvmsg data. So handshake would fail similarly.\n\nWhole-binary fuzz: boot the binary under AFL, feed stdin as the fuzz input. For dhcp6s, stdin isn't used for parsing. Not productive.\n\nThe dependable memory-corruption verification here might be frida — x86 only. Not applicable.\n\nOK. Realistic strategy: static evidence with the strongest dynamic support available:\n- Run trace on dhcp6s (UDP 547). Even though the HTTP GET trigger fails, the trace will confirm the binary runs in qemu (baseline functions) — but \"observed\"/\"verified\" require the diff to show the sink... Not achievable via HTTP GET on a UDP-only daemon.\n\nLet me re-prioritize to finding something verifiable:\n1. httpd - Get a working trace. The issue: qemu-user + httpd accept loop. Maybe httpd uses fork? It's the \"wmnet\" framework: wmnetSocketGroupWait + wmnetTcpAccept. Let me read wmnetTcpAccept/sub_4459C0 and how the request gets read (wmnetTcpRead) and dispatched. If the accept is in the MAIN thread (select loop), then under qemu it should work. The \"Connection reset\" could be because the harness sent HTTP/1.1 GET without \"Connection: close\" and httpd waits for more, then harness closes → httpd sends RST? bytes=0 though.\n\nActually, maybe the trigger request needs to hit a port httpd actually binds IN the sandbox network namespace. qemu-user runs as the sandbox process; binding 0.0.0.0:80 inside a docker container conflicts with nothing. 127.0.0.1:80 in the container → the harness connects to container localhost:80. Should work.\n\nLet me try again with argv [] — real quick run — but the previous 4 attempts all reset. Try one more with a completely different request \"/userRpm/StatusRpm.htm\" to at least see variation; if it resets again, accept that httpd networking doesn't work under this qemu harness and pivot.\n\n2. Meanwhile, use remaining fuzz budget on dhcp6c/dhcp6s? No.\n\nActually wait — what about fuzz targets that ARE startup-reachable:\n- httpd: at startup after init, httpd... no request parsing.\n- BUT minidlna: while startup runs, it does NOT reach HTTP parse. However `readoptionsfile` etc are startup-reachable.\n\nHmm. What about parsing functions of DNS? dnsproxy's dns_decode_request (0x403238)? At startup dnsproxy reads config (conf_cmdparse) — startup reachable! conf_cmdparse parses the config file lines — but that's from file, not network. Actually dnsproxy config comes from /etc or command line, not network.\n\nOK, focus. Let me look closer at the ONE candidate that could be genuinely exploitable and statically provable: dhcp6s/dhcp6c memory corruption in dhcp6_get_options or in the ctl command parsing (base64_decodestring → dhcp6_ctl_readcommand), and the httpd memory bugs.\n\nLet me read dhcp6s's dhcp6_get_options fully (531 lines) — look for missing length checks (e.g., option length larger than buffer, dupid memcpy).\n\nActually, let me be even more direct: the previous finding F-mt4ljkg4-bede was bpalogin stack overflow static-only. The user asks for more with dynamic. Given tool constraints (trace only sends HTTP GET; fuzz only startup-reachable), the practical play:\n\n1. Trace httpd once more (argv [], StatusRpm path) — to HAVE a fresh trace_id with a real run for \"observed\" evidence where possible.\n2. Trace dhcp6s with port 547 (UDP) — confirm at least binary executes (baseline functions) - gives a legit trace_id.\n3. Fuzz: try whole-binary fuzz on minidlnad (only binary_md5) to see if it gets started; if error, note.\n4. Static deep-dive: dhcp6s dhcp6_get_options for a memory bug with a PoC DHCPv6 packet (crafted UDP). And httpd httpBase64decode maybe.\n\nThen record findings as static-only (strong chains + PoC) — that's within the rules (static-only ≤0","stream":true,"block":0}

