— v86[42]=336 bytes! That's the real optinfo buffer (0xE8 = 232 bytes per dhcp6_init_options memset... but v86 is 42*8=336 bytes).\n\nIn the non-relay path: `v8 = v81; dhcp6_init_options(v81);` then `dhcp6_get_options(v112+4, v7, v81)`. Hmm, but if v81 is only 8 bytes and init_options memsets 0xE8 bytes → that would smash the stack massively on EVERY packet. That can't be. So v81 here must be bigger. Let me recount: in sub_4065B4 declarations: `unsigned int v81[2]; // [sp+E8h] [+80h]` — [sp+E8h] with comment [+80h]? The \"[+80h]\" is the offset relative to... something (maybe frame base sp+0x68?). If the frame base for local vars is sp+0x68 (sa array at sp+68), then \"v81 at sp+E8 relative +80h\". Actually these comments mark the offset of the variable within its parent region. The stack is huge (sp+17xx), plenty room. The declared sizes: v86[42] (336B), v90[2], v91[8], v92, v94[168], v95[2], v96[8], v97[8], v99[168], v100[2], v101[8], v102, v103[16], v104[168]... Hmm no single 0xE8 buffer declared for the direct path... v108[46]? That's 184 bytes. Hmm.\n\nActually the compiler merged several buffers. The \"v81[2]\" with comment \"[+80h]\" suggests the decompiler truncated the array size (arrays shown as 2 elements because only accessed via pointer arithmetic a3+N beyond? no, decompiler would show full).\n\nI can't fully resolve the real optinfo size from decompiled views. The sub buffers (v94[168], v99[168], v104[168]) are 168 bytes — those are duid buffers (struct duid in kame dhcp6 = 128 max + header). duidcpy(a3) writing len+ptr — 8 bytes per slot. In sub_4065B4 the flow `duidcpy(v91, &unk_43DE84)` (server DUID copy) — v91[8] = 64 bytes, fine.\n\nI'm going too deep without being able to verify. The important realization: dhcp6_get_options' a3 = \"struct dhcp6_optinfo\" in this binary appears to be a big struct (0xE8=232 bytes per init_options memset). Where is it on the stack? v86[42]=336 bytes in the relay path; the direct path uses v81 which the decompile says [2] — but if it were really 8 bytes, dhcp6_init_options' 0xE8 memset would overflow on every request. Two possibilities: (a) decompiler mis-sized the arrays; (b) real buffer is elsewhere. Given init_options memsets 0xE8 and the relay path passed v86 (336B), the direct path likely passes a similarly-sized buffer that the decompiler printed truncated. Hard to confirm.\n\nAlternative: duidcpy writes pointer+len (8 bytes) — destinations a3[0..1] (client ID) and a3[16..17] (server ID via v98=a3+2? no wait case2 uses v98 = a3+2... hmm case 1 uses a3 directly (0-based), case 2 uses v98=a3+2!).\n\nHold on, re-read: v98 = a3 + 2; case 2: duidcpy(v98, ...) → writes a3[2], a3[3]. But case 1: duidcpy(a3,...) → a3[0], a3[1]. And in sub_4065B4, client ID check is `if ( !v81[0] )` (no client ID option → v81[0] is the len). Server ID check `if ( !v82[0] )` — v82 separate array. So case2 (server ID) writes to a3+2 which corresponds to v82[0..1] IF v82 follows v81 contiguously. In stack layout sp+E8 (v81) then sp+F0 (v82) — YES contiguous 8+8. So the optinfo struct low part = {client_duid {len,ptr}, server_duid {len,ptr}} = 16 bytes spanning v81+v82. The rest (a3+4.. a3+55) reference... a3+4 = sp+F0+... hmm v82 ends at F8. a3[4] would be at F8 — next declared var v83 at F8. OK so the whole optinfo struct spans many adjacent locals (v81..v108 region). The decompiler just split them into locals.\n\nSo duidcpy writes 8 bytes (len + pointer) — no overflow. The DUID data itself is heap-allocated via malloc(v7). Safe.\n\nOK dhcp6_get_options is not obviously vulnerable.\n\nLet me step back and think about what's most productive in remaining budget:\n\n1. Remaining fuzz attempts (6 max, used 1): try whole-binary fuzz on minidlnad — instructions say if function-level handshake fails, go whole binary. Let me do that now: fw_request_fuzz binary_md5 only (minidlna), seconds 120.\n\n2. Trace httpd once more with argv [] (empty) and a StatusRpm path — to have fresh evidence. Actually, we already have multiple traces with httpd. No need to burn another.\n\n3. Trace dhcp6s (binary md5 23e37960, port 547) — baseline run to see if it even starts (needs interface arg in argv: dhcp6s <interface>). argv [\"eth0\"]? Under qemu, if_nametoindex will fail → exits. It'd still give baseline functions. This gives a trace_id for dhcp6s runs. But \"observed\" would require sink execution; not happening. I'll still run it to document dynamic attempt, but findings recorded as static-only.\n\nBetter use of the trace budget: try minidlna with argv to run foreground and see if it listens: this time argv [\"-f\",\"-d\",\"-c\",\"/tmp/minidlna.conf\"?] — no config file present. Let me check min","stream":true,"block":0}

