data: {"seq":629,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:20:06.227Z","text":"Clarification:\n- duidcpy: `*a1 = len; a1[1] = malloc(len); memcpy(a1[1], src, len)` — it writes len and a pointer into a1[0], a1[1] (8 bytes total). So from dhcp6_get_options case 1: duidcpy(a3, &v64) writes a3[0]=len, a3[1]=ptr. a3 here... in sub_4065B4, dhcp6_get_options(..., v81) with v81[2] = 8 bytes exactly! a3[0], a3[1] = 8 bytes. That fits. And v82 = a3+16 → a3[16], a3[17] — outside the 8-byte v81!! Wait, v82 = _DWORD[2] declared separately in sub_4065B4: `_DWORD v82[2];` so v82 is a separate 8-byte array. In dhcp6_get_options: v98 = a3 + 2 → case 2 duidcpy(v98) writes a3[2], a3[3] = 8 bytes at offset 8..16 of a3 — again within v81's 8 bytes? No! v81 is only 8 bytes (indices 0..1). a3[2], a3[3] would be at bytes 8..16 → overflow of v81 by 8 bytes into v82 (adjacent). Hmm, but v82 is `_DWORD v82[2]` in sub_4065B4 at [sp+F0h], and v81[2] at [sp+E8h]. So v81 is 8 bytes (E8..EF), v82 is at F0..F7. a3[2] = bytes 8..11 of v81 → that overlaps... wait a3 = base aligned at E8. a3[2] at E8+8 = F0 = v82[0]. So case 2 (server ID) duidcpy(v98=a3+2) writes v82[0], v82[1] = F0..F7 = exactly v82's 8 bytes! Coincidence? Actually that means the intended layout is: the optinfo struct spans both v81 and v82 arrays (v81=8 bytes + v82=8 bytes contiguous). But hmm, v83.. etc.? In the compacted decompile, offsets beyond are handled by other locals. This is a \"size_t *a3\" pointing at size_t[2] but with fields at a3+2, a3+4... reaching a3+55!! a3[55] is byte offset 440 from a3! But v81/v82 only give 16 bytes on the 4065B4 stack!\n\nWait — I need to recheck. In sub_4065B4, the call was `dhcp6_get_options((unsigned __int8 *)(v112 + 4), (unsigned int)v7, v81)` — but which v81? Looking at the declarations: `_DWORD v82[2];` at sp+F0 and `unsigned int v81[2]` at sp+E8. BUT ALSO `size_t v86[42]` at sp+1D0 and `_DWORD v87[2]` sp+278... The first call in the relay path was `dhcp6_init_options(v86); dhcp6_get_options(v19+34, v7, v86)` — v86[42]=336 bytes! That's the real optinfo buffer (0xE8 = 232 bytes per dhcp6_init_options memset... but v86 is 42*8=336 bytes).\n\nIn the non-relay path: `v8 = v81; dhcp6_init_options(v81);` then `dhcp6_get_options(v112+4, v7, v81)`. Hmm, but if v81 is only 8 bytes and init_options memsets 0xE8 bytes → that would smash the stack massively on EVERY packet. That can't be. So v81 here must be bigger. Let me recount: in sub_4065B4 declarations: `unsigned int v81[2]; // [sp+E8h] [+80h]` — [sp+E8h] with comment [+80h]? The \"[+80h]\" is the offset relative to... something (maybe frame base sp+0x68?). If the frame base for local vars is sp+0x68 (sa array at sp+68), then \"v81 at sp+E8 relative +80h\". Actually these comments mark the offset of the variable within its parent region. The stack is huge (sp+17xx), plenty room. The declared sizes: v86[42] (336B), v90[2], v91[8], v92, v94[168], v95[2], v96[8], v97[8], v99[168], v100[2], v101[8], v102, v103[16], v104[168]... Hmm no single 0xE8 buffer declared for the direct path... v108[46]? That's 184 bytes. Hmm.\n\nActually the compiler merged several buffers. The \"v81[2]\" with comment \"[+80h]\" suggests the decompiler truncated the array size (arrays shown as 2 elements because only accessed via pointer arithmetic a3+N beyond? no, decompiler would show full).\n\nI can't fully resolve the real optinfo size from decompiled views. The sub buffers (v94[168], v99[168], v104[168]) are 168 bytes — those are duid buffers (struct duid in kame dhcp6 = 128 max + header). duidcpy(a3) writing len+ptr — 8 bytes per slot. In sub_4065B4 the flow `duidcpy(v91, &unk_43DE84)` (server DUID copy) — v91[8] = 64 bytes, fine.\n\nI'm going too deep without being able to verify. The important realization: dhcp6_get_options' a3 = \"struct dhcp6_optinfo\" in this binary appears to be a big struct (0xE8=232 bytes per init_options memset). Where is it on the stack? v86[42]=336 bytes in the relay path; the direct path uses v81 which the decompile says [2] — but if it were really 8 bytes, dhcp6_init_options' 0xE8 memset would overflow on every request. Two possibilities: (a) decompiler mis-sized the arrays; (b) real buffer is elsewhere. Given init_options memsets 0xE8 and the relay path passed v86 (336B), the direct path likely passes a similarly-sized buffer that the decompiler printed truncated. Hard to confirm.\n\nAlternative: duidcpy writes pointer+len (8 bytes) — destinations a3[0..1] (client ID) and a3[16..17] (server ID via v98=a3+2? no wait case2 uses v98 = a3+2... hmm case 1 uses a3 directly (0-based), case 2 uses v98=a3+2!).\n\nHold on, re-read: v98 = a3 + 2; case 2: duidcpy(v98, ...) → writes a3[2], a3[3]. But case 1: duidcpy(a3,...) → a3[0], a3[1]. And in sub_4065B4, client ID check is `if ( !v81[0] )` (no client ID option → v81[0] is the len). Server ID check `if ( !v82[0] )` — v82 separate array. So case2 (server ID) writes to a3+2 which corresponds to v82[0..1] IF v82 follows v81 contiguously. In stack layout sp+E8 (v81) then sp+F0 (v82) — YES contiguous 8+8. So the optinfo struct low part = {client_duid {len,ptr}, server_duid {len,ptr}} = 16 bytes spanning v81+v82. The rest (a3+4.. a3+55) reference... a3+4 = sp+F0+... hmm v82 ends at F8. a3[4] would be at F8 — next declared var v83 at F8. OK so the whole optinfo struct spans many adjacent locals (v81..v108 region). The decompiler just split them into locals.\n\nSo duidcpy writes 8 bytes (len + pointer) — no overflow. The DUID data itself is heap-allocated via malloc(v7). Safe.\n\nOK dhcp6_get_options is not obviously vulnerable.\n\nLet me step back and think about what's most productive in remaining budget:\n\n1. Remaining fuzz attempts (6 max, used 1): try whole-binary fuzz on minidlnad — instructions say if function-level handshake fails, go whole binary. Let me do that now: fw_request_fuzz binary_md5 only (minidlna), seconds 120.\n\n2. Trace httpd once more with argv [] (empty) and a StatusRpm path — to have fresh evidence. Actually, we already have multiple traces with httpd. No need to burn another.\n\n3. Trace dhcp6s (binary md5 23e37960, port 547) — baseline run to see if it even starts (needs interface arg in argv: dhcp6s <interface>). argv [\"eth0\"]? Under qemu, if_nametoindex will fail → exits. It'd still give baseline functions. This gives a trace_id for dhcp6s runs. But \"observed\" would require sink execution; not happening. I'll still run it to document dynamic attempt, but findings recorded as static-only.\n\nBetter use of the trace budget: try minidlna with argv to run foreground and see if it listens: this time argv [\"-f\",\"-d\",\"-c\",\"/tmp/minidlna.conf\"?] — no config file present. Let me check minidlna's readoptionsfile default and whether missing config → exit. Hmm.\n\nGiven minidlna UPNP-over-HTTP needs a config, the earlier c6bb8ac732fb run with \"-d -i lo\" got 47 functions. Let me re-run minidlna with \"-f -d\" (no -i) port 8200 — maybe works. Actually we have limited trace budget (12); already ~10 exist. Let me check: used traces listed 10 total; budget says max 12 per session. Trace IDs so far: c6bb8ac732fb, 3b662344b97a, b13c28e670b7, 157e1f694b18, 93b94696d1f5, 4cfb0cac0b5c, 2ba400f78c44, ef55266d1f34, 8519f7adc93d, 2225718d1472 = 10, plus 822e53e6595c = 11. Only 1 left. Use it wisely: dhcp6s? or minidlna config attempt?\n\nThe most informative last trace: minidlna with \"-f -d -p 8200\" to see if it binds port 8200. If refused again → daemon exits before listen under this qemu env (config issue), confirming dynamic limits.\n\nActually — wait. Maybe make minidlna listen by providing argv pointing to a config file that EXISTS in the rootfs. Check for *.conf files anywhere. Let me search rootfs for minidlna conf: browse etc (empty earlier!). The \"etc\" dict listing didn't happen — I got \"entries\":[] for etc. Actually I browsed etc earlier and got entries of etc... no, the earlier `etc` browse returned entries []?? No wait — the first `etc` browse returned \"entries\": [] — that was wrong path? No, I called path \"etc\" and got entries []... but later path \"firmware/.../etc\" listed files. The root browse root is different (emic data dir). OK: the true etc has no minidlna.conf.\n\nDecision: last trace = dhcp6s (UDP port 547) with argv [\"eth0\"]: it will exercise main → cfparse(off_43C078[0]=NULL config?) Actually -c not given → off_43C078[0] = NULL? cfparse(NULL) → reads default /etc/dhcp6s.conf (exists? no). It may exit early — fine, baseline functions recorded. This at least demonstrates dhcp6s qemu behavior.\n\nHmm, however — is that worth the last trace slot? I think yes: user explicitly wants fw_request_trace/fw_get_trace usage on dhcp6s.\n\nLet me also do the whole-binary fuzz for minidlnad now (fuzz budget separate, 6 max, used 1).\n\nAnd in parallel continue static: the httpd memory-corruption hunt. Let me look at httpd's httpRpmFs handler registration: how do \"sw_*\" handlers get invoked from HTTP GETs? Maybe there's an unauth'd path. But given the huge function count, let me target known weak spots: httpBase64decode out-of-bounds read? bounded. httpMimeParse? Let me check `httpMimeParse` @ 0x444940 and `sub_420B48` (the parse); these parse the request line into buffer; look for stack overflows (e.g., strcpy of URL into fixed buffer).\n\nActually — better: attack surface score list might include httpd paths deeper. Let me query fw_attack_surface with verified_only=false and check the paths: earlier list showed dhcp6s/xl2tpd/libavformat-centric top-20. httpd paths might be lower-scored. Let me look for httpd binary paths: fetch with limit 50 and scan for md5 9582aa23.","stream":true,"block":0}

