data: {"seq":650,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:20:16.468Z","text":"The top-50 attack surface is dominated by dhcp6s/xl2tpd/libavformat(01312699). No httpd paths in the top. libavformat ff_rtsp_connect strcpy (b30fcc1f..., 6.05) — a strcpy from offset s[1601] into dest with network data — that's in libavformat used by minidlna? No — minidlna uses libavformat for media probing of LOCAL files (ffmpeg avformat_open_input on NAS files), and RTSP connect is only used if the media URL is rtsp:// (config). Not HTTP-reachable.\n\nOK. So per the graph, the strongest *network* candidates are:\n1. dhcp6s dm_duid path (P0) — already shown not network-reachable (debug echo w/ argv).\n2. xl2tpd network_thread strcpy (P0 bof) — real network (UDP 1701) but excluded service.\n\nHmm, wait — before dismissing xl2tpd: on this firmware, is xl2tpd actually listening on WAN? identification EXCLUDED it (not-public). Also start_pppd execv not injection. network_thread strcpy at j+916 — where does v10 come from? If it's the L2TP control packet hostname AVP — attacker-controlled (anyone can send UDP to 1701 if WAN-side L2TP server mode). But excluded by the gate. The user said continue httpd/minidlna/dhcp6s \"等\" — i.e., priority list. I'll note xl2tpd as a lead but not file it (excluded).\n\nActually — since F-mt4ljkg4-bede was bpalogin and it's already recorded, and given all top network paths are in excluded/argv-only services, the realistic deliverables this round:\n\n1. dhcp6s sub_40F0F8 sys_run_command: vsprintf stack overflow potential (260-byte) but only reached from get_duid debug echo path with argv-derived interface name and getifaddrs data. Given main→get_duid(0x40f1d0)→sub_40F168→sub_40F0F8, and interface name is argv[optind] — NOT attacker-controlled over network. Not a network vuln.","stream":true,"block":0}

