data: {"seq":683,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:20:31.270Z","text":"sub_40D1FC analysis for memory bugs:\n\n- Method: reads up to 15 chars into v7[16] ✓ bounded.\n- URL: reads up to 511 chars into v8[7]!!! Wait — v8 is `char v8[7];` but the loop is `for (i=0; i<511 && *v5!=32 && *v5!=13; ++i) v8[i] = *v5++;` writing i up to 510 into a 7-byte buffer!! Classic stack overflow!\n\nHold on — is v8 really 7 bytes? `char v8[7]; // [sp+50h] [-?]` Yes: v8[7] at sp+50, then v9[2] at sp+57, v10 at sp+59, v11[2] at sp+5A, v12[500] at sp+5C... So v8 is 7 bytes at sp+50..56. The URL copy loop writes 511 bytes starting at sp+50 → overwrites v9(2), v10(1), v11(2), v12(500), src..., and beyond — up to 511 bytes from sp+50 to sp+50+511 = sp+26F. src at sp+250, v14 at 254, v15 at 258..? That's all overwritten, and beyond sp+26F into saved regs/fp/ra.\n\nWait, but the decompiler might be undersizing v8. This looks like the stock minidlna code:\n\n```c\nchar command[16];\nchar httpver[16];\nchar url[1024];\n...\nfor (i = 0; i < 511 && ...; i++) url[i] = ...\n```\n\nIn stock minidlna upnphttp.c GetHTTPResponse:\n```c\nchar command[16];\nchar httpver[16];\nchar url[1024];\n```\nHmm — actually stock:\n```c\n\tcommand[0] = '\\0'; url[0] = '\\0'; ...\n\tfor (i = 0; i < 15 && p[0] != ' ' && p[0] != '\\r'; i++) command[i] = *p++;\n...\n\tfor (i = 0; i < 511 && p[0] != ' ' && p[0] != '\\r'; i++) url[i] = *p++;\n```\n\nAnd url is declared as `char url[1024];`? Not sure. If v8 is really 7 bytes in this build, that's a huge overflow. But decompiler stack offsets: sp+50 with size 7 — could be misread because the array is followed immediately by other small arrays. The loop `i<511` strongly suggests url buffer intended 512+. Then v8 might actually be `char v8[512]`? But v12[500] at sp+5C and v8 is BEFORE v12 (lower address sp+50 < sp+5C). If v8 were 512 bytes it would overlap v12... The layout suggests v8 is REALLY small here OR the decompiler shows the tail of a bigger buffer.\n\nActually wait — look at SendResp_thumbnail((int*)a1, v12) — v12 (500 bytes at sp+5C) is passed as the HTTP RESPONSE buffer for thumbnail handler, and SendResp_dlnafile((int*)a1, v12) too. So v12 is a stack buffer used to hold resp data. v8 (URL) is 7 bytes?? That can't be the real URL buffer used elsewhere... but sub_40D050(a1, v8) (UNSUBSCRIBE), sub_40CCC8(a1, v8) (SUBSCRIBE) use v8 as URI. And SendResp paths use v12.\n\nGiven i<511 bound writes into v8 region: from sp+50, 511 bytes → sp+26F. The frame: what's saved there","stream":true,"block":0}

