data: {"seq":702,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:20:38.421Z","text":"No asm export. Let me try fw_get_cfg for the function to see the basic blocks, or fw_get_ast to understand structure. Actually, better: check stock minidlna source for this exact function name set (this is TP-Link's port of minidlna ~1.0.x). In stock minidlna upnphttp.c:\n\n```c\nstatic void\nGetHTTPResponse(struct upnphttp * h)\n{\n\tchar command[16];\n\tchar httpver[16];\n\tchar url[1024];\n\tchar path[1024];\n\t...\n\tconst char * p;\n\t...\n\tp = ... h->req_buf...\n\t// command\n\tfor (i = 0; i < 15 && p[0] != ' ' && p[0] != '\\r'; i++)\n\t\tcommand[i] = *p++;\n\tcommand[i] = '\\0';\n\t...\n\t// url\n\tfor (i=0; i < 511 && p[0] != ' ' && p[0] != '\\r'; i++)\n\t\turl[i] = *p++;\n```\n\nHmm stock uses `char url[512]`? Actually minidlna 1.1.2:\n```\n\tchar url[1024];\n\tchar path[1024];\n```\nHmm. TP-Link's build might differ.\n\nThe decompiler naming v8[7] might indicate a mis-decompiled huge buffer whose visible portion got broken at offsets where other stack objects sit. But the offsets: v7[16] at sp+40 (command, 16 bytes — matches stock command[16]!). v8 at sp+50 (url?), v9[2] at sp+57, v10 at sp+59, v11[2] at sp+5A, v12[500] at sp+5C (path[500]?).\n\nStock:\n```\nchar command[16];\nchar httpver[16];\nchar url[1024];\nchar path[512];\n```\nHmm, that doesn't map: if url were at sp+50 it would be 1024 bytes → sp+450, overlapping v12 at sp+5C. Impossible.\n\nUnless... v8 is actually just 7 bytes and there IS a real bug. The loop bound 511 vs buffer 7. Let me look at where these v-buffers get USED:\n- v8 → passed to sub_40D050(a1, v8) (UNSUBSCRIBE handler) and sub_40CCC8 (SUBSCRIBE).\n- v12 → passed to SendResp_thumbnail / SendResp_dlnafile as second arg (probably output buffer for \"object id\" parsing?).\n\nWait — actually in stock minidlna the \"url\" parse stores the path;","stream":true,"block":0}

