data: {"seq":724,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:20:46.308Z","text":"dhcp6s exits immediately with argv eth0 (rc=0) — ifinit(\"eth0\") fails or exits (in qemu, eth0 doesn't exist). It exited rc=0 cleanly early. This is dynamic evidence that dhcp6s requires a real interface and won't run under qemu-user without fake network. Note: dhcp6c/dhcp6s also need lease/db files.\n\nLet me wait for the fuzz. Meanwhile, let me reconsider what solid finding to record. We have:\n\n1. dhcp6s main (0x407ff8) → get_duid (0x40f1d0) → sub_40F168 → sub_40F0F8: vsprintf(s[260], \"%s\", ...) + system. get_duid passes interface name (argv[3rd param]=v14=argv[optind]) into sub_40CE98 but sub_40F168 is called with CONSTANT aEth02. So the format string args aren't attacker-controlled. The stack overflow in sub_40F168's vsprintf: s[260] with format \"[%s]wan_if:%s\\n\" args \"get_duid\" + aEth02 (constant). Not controllable. NOT a real vuln. Good analysis to exclude.\n\n2. dhcp6c client6_recvreply: sprintf(s[128], \"echo %s %s >> /tmp/debugInfo\", \"client6_recvreply\", dhcp6_stcodestr(status)) → system(s). dhcp6_stcodestr uses snprintf into global with \"%d\" → \"status code\" numeric string, length ≤ ~7. So format is safe: max \"echo client6_recvreply 99999 >> /tmp/debugInfo\" — fine. BUT: is the *status* value bounded? __int16 status from option → snprintf(\"%d\", value) max 6 chars. Safe. Not a vuln.\n\n3. What about dhcp6c/6s control channel? dhcp6_ctl_readcommand reads from control socket and the data is base64-decoded then","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":725,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:20:46.698Z","text":"dhcp6s exits immediately with argv eth0 (rc=0) — ifinit(\"eth0\") fails or exits (in qemu, eth0 doesn't exist). It exited rc=0 cleanly early. This is dynamic evidence that dhcp6s requires a real interface and won't run under qemu-user without fake network. Note: dhcp6c/dhcp6s also need lease/db files.\n\nLet me wait for the fuzz. Meanwhile, let me reconsider what solid finding to record. We have:\n\n1. dhcp6s main (0x407ff8) → get_duid (0x40f1d0) → sub_40F168 → sub_40F0F8: vsprintf(s[260], \"%s\", ...) + system. get_duid passes interface name (argv[3rd param]=v14=argv[optind]) into sub_40CE98 but sub_40F168 is called with CONSTANT aEth02. So the format string args aren't attacker-controlled. The stack overflow in sub_40F168's vsprintf: s[260] with format \"[%s]wan_if:%s\\n\" args \"get_duid\" + aEth02 (constant). Not controllable. NOT a real vuln. Good analysis to exclude.\n\n2. dhcp6c client6_recvreply: sprintf(s[128], \"echo %s %s >> /tmp/debugInfo\", \"client6_recvreply\", dhcp6_stcodestr(status)) → system(s). dhcp6_stcodestr uses snprintf into global with \"%d\" → \"status code\" numeric string, length ≤ ~7. So format is safe: max \"echo client6_recvreply 99999 >> /tmp/debugInfo\" — fine. BUT: is the *status* value bounded? __int16 status from option → snprintf(\"%d\", value) max 6 chars. Safe. Not a vuln.\n\n3. What about dhcp6c/6s control channel? dhcp6_ctl_readcommand reads from control socket and the data is base64-decoded then dispatched to sub_404BE0 (dhcp6c) which does dhcp6_verify_mac(auto…) and cfparse... The control message REINIT: in dhcp6c, \"reconfigure\" control messages trigger... The ctl channel is authenticated with HMAC (dhcp6_ctl_authinit with keyfile /etc/dhcp6cctlkey). The key file exists in firmware","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":726,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:20:47.055Z","text":"dhcp6s exits immediately with argv eth0 (rc=0) — ifinit(\"eth0\") fails or exits (in qemu, eth0 doesn't exist). It exited rc=0 cleanly early. This is dynamic evidence that dhcp6s requires a real interface and won't run under qemu-user without fake network. Note: dhcp6c/dhcp6s also need lease/db files.\n\nLet me wait for the fuzz. Meanwhile, let me reconsider what solid finding to record. We have:\n\n1. dhcp6s main (0x407ff8) → get_duid (0x40f1d0) → sub_40F168 → sub_40F0F8: vsprintf(s[260], \"%s\", ...) + system. get_duid passes interface name (argv[3rd param]=v14=argv[optind]) into sub_40CE98 but sub_40F168 is called with CONSTANT aEth02. So the format string args aren't attacker-controlled. The stack overflow in sub_40F168's vsprintf: s[260] with format \"[%s]wan_if:%s\\n\" args \"get_duid\" + aEth02 (constant). Not controllable. NOT a real vuln. Good analysis to exclude.\n\n2. dhcp6c client6_recvreply: sprintf(s[128], \"echo %s %s >> /tmp/debugInfo\", \"client6_recvreply\", dhcp6_stcodestr(status)) → system(s). dhcp6_stcodestr uses snprintf into global with \"%d\" → \"status code\" numeric string, length ≤ ~7. So format is safe: max \"echo client6_recvreply 99999 >> /tmp/debugInfo\" — fine. BUT: is the *status* value bounded? __int16 status from option → snprintf(\"%d\", value) max 6 chars. Safe. Not a vuln.\n\n3. What about dhcp6c/6s control channel? dhcp6_ctl_readcommand reads from control socket and the data is base64-decoded then dispatched to sub_404BE0 (dhcp6c) which does dhcp6_verify_mac(auto…) and cfparse... The control message REINIT: in dhcp6c, \"reconfigure\" control messages trigger... The ctl channel is authenticated with HMAC (dhcp6_ctl_authinit with keyfile /etc/dhcp6cctlkey). The key file exists in firmware. So control channel requires the key → local admin.\n\nOK, so for the named targets, real exploitable holes require more digging into httpd/uclited. Let me examine uclited's execFormatCmd callers — the nas storage format — who calls it and with what? Search callers of execFormatCmd.","stream":true,"block":0}

