data: {"seq":806,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:21:24.095Z","text":"execWlanCmd: many sprintf→system with a2/a3 arguments. Case 7/15/21 wrap the string in single quotes with '→\"' escape logic (v21 buffer 132 bytes, input <33 for case 7, <??? for 15/21 — v7 loop caps at v4=len (unbounded up to strlen!), writing 3 bytes per input char into v21[132] → overflow possible for len>44!). Case 15: `while (v11 < v8)` with v8=strlen(a3) — no length check! v21[132] → writes 3*v8 bytes → if a3 length > 44 → stack overflow of v21. Also, quoting is weak (quotes can contain `';` sequences since embedded `'` becomes `\"'` — shell still interprets string concatenation; injection like `x';touch /tmp/pwn;'` remains possible: input `';` → output `'';'` hmm let's think: input char `'` → becomes `\"'` (doublequote singlequote doublequote). Input \"';ls;'\" → v21 = \"'\"'\";ls;\"''\" ... complicated but classic TP-Link injections bypass via `$(...)` inside the quoted arg: input `$(cmd)` → wrapped as `'$(cmd)'` — $() DOES expand inside single quotes? NO — inside single quotes, $() is NOT expanded by shell. Wait but their quoting puts single quotes around... ugh, the transformation: for each char c: output `'c'` (with ' becoming `\"'`). So \"a;b\" → \"'a'';''b'\" — inside, ; is inside single quotes → not a command separator. Actually each char individually wrapped in single quotes → the shell sees a concatenation of single-quoted single chars: `'a'';''b'` = literal \"a;b\". So it's actually SAFE against ; injection (every char individually quoted). But the overflow: case 15/21 unbounded v21[132] overflow with long a3!","stream":true,"block":0}

