data: {"seq":828,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:21:33.253Z","text":"ProcessSSDPRequest:\n- recvfrom into v36[1500] with 0x5DB (1499) — then `v36[v35]=0` — v35 ≤ 1499, ok.\n- NOTIFY path: haystack (LOCATION value) → sub_420E40(haystack) if \"MediaRenderer\" NT + matches. sub_420E40 is \"net_open_connection\" per surface — it opens HTTP connection to LOCATION URL (to fetch SamsungMRDesc.xml!). So a malicious SSDP NOTIFY from LAN","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":829,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:21:33.617Z","text":"ProcessSSDPRequest:\n- recvfrom into v36[1500] with 0x5DB (1499) — then `v36[v35]=0` — v35 ≤ 1499, ok.\n- NOTIFY path: haystack (LOCATION value) → sub_420E40(haystack) if \"MediaRenderer\" NT + matches. sub_420E40 is \"net_open_connection\" per surface — it opens HTTP connection to LOCATION URL (to fetch SamsungMRDesc.xml!). So a malicious SSDP NOTIFY from LAN could make minidlna fetch an arbitrary HTTP URL (attacker-controlled), then parse XML from it. That's SSRF-ish, not memory corruption directly, but the XML parser then processes attacker-controlled content — potentially exploitable.\n\nInteresting, but time is limited. Priority:","stream":true,"block":0}

