data: {"seq":985,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:22:48.238Z","text":"dhcp6_ctl_init: uses getaddrinfo(name, service, ...) then socket + bind + listen — with `name`/`service` args. In dhcp6c main, ctl init call: dhcp6_ctl_init(off_43C070 [ctlkeyfile], off_43C074 [ctlport], ...). In KAME dhcp6, dhcp6_ctl_init(\"\", \"5470\") for TCP control channel! Actually YES — dhcp6 uses a TCP control channel (4870/5470) with HMAC-MD5 auth. `dhcp6_ctl_init(name, service...)` → getaddrinfo name → bind → listen → TCP. So dhcp6c/dhcp6s control ports ARE TCP (default 5470).\n\nFor dhcp6s: `dhcp6_ctl_init(off_43C070, off_43C074, 5, &dword_43C060)` — off_43C070 = ctlkeyfile (default /etc/dhcp6sctlkey, exists!), off_43C074 = ctlport (default 5470). BUT in dhcp6s main, ctl init only if dword_43C608 (auth key loaded OK?). If keyfile open fails → dword_43C608=0 → \"skip opening control port\". In qemu, /etc/dhcp6sctlkey exists... so control port opens on 5470/tcp?\n\nThe control channel is HMAC-authenticated (dhcp6_verify_mac with the key). Attacker without the key can't issue commands (replay of captured? replay could reissue commands!). Still, the ctl protocol on TCP","stream":true,"block":0}

