data: {"seq":989,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:22:49.766Z","text":"dhcp6_ctl_init: uses getaddrinfo(name, service, ...) then socket + bind + listen — with `name`/`service` args. In dhcp6c main, ctl init call: dhcp6_ctl_init(off_43C070 [ctlkeyfile], off_43C074 [ctlport], ...). In KAME dhcp6, dhcp6_ctl_init(\"\", \"5470\") for TCP control channel! Actually YES — dhcp6 uses a TCP control channel (4870/5470) with HMAC-MD5 auth. `dhcp6_ctl_init(name, service...)` → getaddrinfo name → bind → listen → TCP. So dhcp6c/dhcp6s control ports ARE TCP (default 5470).\n\nFor dhcp6s: `dhcp6_ctl_init(off_43C070, off_43C074, 5, &dword_43C060)` — off_43C070 = ctlkeyfile (default /etc/dhcp6sctlkey, exists!), off_43C074 = ctlport (default 5470). BUT in dhcp6s main, ctl init only if dword_43C608 (auth key loaded OK?). If keyfile open fails → dword_43C608=0 → \"skip opening control port\". In qemu, /etc/dhcp6sctlkey exists... so control port opens on 5470/tcp?\n\nThe control channel is HMAC-authenticated (dhcp6_verify_mac with the key). Attacker without the key can't issue commands (replay of captured? replay could reissue commands!). Still, the ctl protocol on TCP 5470 with HMAC is the classic kame dhcp6 design; commands like \"reconfig\"/\"info\" — if an attacker can send a VALID HMAC (key in /etc/dhcp6cctlkey shared on device, static per firmware?) — the key file content is in the firmware! Let me check /etc/dhcp6sctlkey content — if it's a static shared key, anyone who extracts the firmware can authenticate to the control channel. Let me look at the key file. It's likely 128 hex chars. If static and non-rotating per device, then the control channel is effectively unauthenticated against attackers who know the public firmware key!\n\nLet me check the key file + how dhcp6_ctl_readcommand handles commands (\"reconfigure\", \"info\") and what sub_404BE0 does (in dhcp6c) — reload config & REINIT? If we","stream":true,"block":0}

