data: {"seq":1019,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:23:01.283Z","text":"Both ctl keys are STATIC base64 strings in the firmware (Sw5j... and mHg8...). Base64 of 16 bytes = 128-bit HMAC-MD5 keys, embedded in every device of this model. Anyone with firmware access knows the key. So the DHCPv6 control channels (TCP 5470 on dhcp6s / dhcp6c) can be spoken by any remote attacker who knows the key... BUT the control channel listens where? dhcp6_ctl_init(name, service) — name param in main is off_43C070 = \"-k\" ctlkeyfile... wait name here = keyfile path, service = ctlport. getaddrinfo(name=keyfile?, ...) — hmm, getaddrinfo(name, service) with name being the KEYFILE string would be a hostname?! Recheck: main: `dhcp6_ctl_init(off_43C070, off_43C074, 5, &dword_43C060)`; off_43C070 = optarg of -k (ctlkeyfile)! So name=\"-k value\" = keyfile... then dhcp6_ctl_init uses name for getaddrinfo? That would be wrong — keyfile can't be hostname. Actually the signature is (char *name, char *service, int a3, int *a4) where a3=5 (probably protocol/authtype) and a4=out socket. In kame dhcp6 code: dhcp6_ctl_init(const char *name, const char *service, int authentication, int *ctl_sock) — name/service used via getaddrinfo(NULL? or name=hostname). For dhcp6c: dhcp6_ctl_init(NULL, DHCP6CTL_PORT...)? Here they pass keyfile path as name?? No wait — the -k option sets off_43C070 AND off_43C07C (auth keyfile for dhcp6_ctl_authinit). Looking at dhcp6s main: `dhcp6_ctl_authinit(off_43C07C[0], &dword_43C608, &dword_43DE94)` (key used for auth init), and `dhcp6_ctl_init(off_43C070, off_43C074, 5, &dword_43C060)` — off_43C070 is... from getopt: `case 'c': off_43C078[0] = optarg` (config); `case 'k': off_43C07C[0] = optarg` (keyfile); `case 'p': off_43C074 = optarg` (ctlport). What sets off_43C070? Hmm — off_43C070 wasn't set by any option in the getopt loop I saw! Only c/d/f/k/n/p/P/D. So off_43C070 = NULL (bss zero). dhcp6_ctl_init(NULL, \"5470\", 5, &fd) → getaddrinfo(NULL host, port 5470) → bind 0.0.0.0:5470? or ::5470. Interesting — BIND ANY ADDRESS:5470 TCP!\n\nSo dhcp6s (when key present at /etc/dhcp6sctlkey and dword_43C608 nonzero... wait ctl init happens if dword_43C608 (authinit ok)). authinit reads keyfile: if keyfile exists → dword_43C608=1 → ctl channel opens TCP 0.0.0.0:5470 with that static key!\n\nReaching: any network attacker knowing the static key can connect TCP 5470 and issue authenticated control commands. What commands? dhcp6_ctl_readcommand parses: type 0=command, 1=reconfigure, 2=relay... KAME dhcp6ctl commands: INFO, RECONF... Actually the dhcp6s ctl: \"reconfigure\" (reload config), \"info\". Reloading config with an attacker... no file control. Limited impact — DoS (reload) at most, unless a command leads to code exec.\n\nGiven time budget, this static-key finding is \"weak\" for exploitation. Not record-worthy without deeper command analysis.\n\nFINAL DECISION — I must record at least the solid ones. Defensible findings to record now:\n\n1. uclited execWlanCmd case 15/21 stack overflow — static-only, CWE-121, medium. Call chain: HTTP POST(认证) → httpd swWlan* → loopback ucm(127.0.0.1:51237) → ucm_cfg_handler → ucWlanSecurityConfigSet/载入配置 → execWlanCmd(0x49c84c) → v21[132] 溢出（每字符 3 字节无边界检查）。PoC: 63 字符 WPA Passphrase。confidence ~0.45 (chain entry未完全证实到 execWlanCmd 调用点)。Hmm — call chain needs function names+addrs connected by →. My chain has a gap (\"ucm_cfg_handler → ...执行无线配置应用 → execWlanCmd\"). Too speculative? The call_trace returned no callers; the in_degree=1 for execWlanCmd means the graph HAS one caller but I didn't get its name. Recording with a gap is weak.\n\nLet me try ONE more search to find the caller: search \"execWlanCmd\" gave only the function. Try to find which functions reference 0x49c84c: not searchable. Try search pattern \"WlanCmd\" broader:\n\nActually — alternatives. Let me check \"apply\" functions in uclited: pattern \"Apply\".","stream":true,"block":0}

