data: {"seq":1365,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:30:59.382Z","text":"KEY FIND in nasSambaSetShareList (uclited 0x4a63ec):\n\n```c\nv2 = *(_DWORD *)(a1 + 76);   // index into inner-samba share list?\nif ( v2 )\n    snprintf(v6, 0x80, \"rm -rf /tmp/samba/%s.smb.conf\", (const char *)(a1 + 80));\nelse\n    snprintf(v6, 0x80, \"rm -rf /tmp/samba/%s.smb.conf\", (const char *)(a1 + 256));\nsystem(v6);\n```\n\nThe share-name string at a1+80 or a1+256 is interpolated into a **shell command** (`rm -rf /tmp/samba/<name>.smb.conf`). If share name contains shell metacharacters (e.g., `x;telnetd -l /bin/sh;.smb.conf`), we get **command injection as root**!\n\nWhere does (a1+80)/(a1+256) come from? a1 is the Samba config structure filled from the ucm set-op (`nasSambaSetShareList` is reached via ucm cfg handler from httpd's NAS share settings POST \"NasShareSettingsRpm.htm\" or similar). TP-Link typically does NOT sanitize share names on this generation (there IS a known TP-Link CVE family here: e.g., CVE-2020-8957/8958 are about TL-WR902AC? and \"Archer C7 smb.conf share name command injection\" — actually yes! CVE-2020-... \"TP-Link Archer C7 share name command injection\" is a real published bug!).\n\nLet me verify where a1+80/a1+256 strings are set: they should be the share name stored from the HTTP param \"shareName\" (via doType=config & shareEntry). Also sub_4A5EEC etc. build share dirs.\n\nNow: is this reachable UNAUTHENTICATED? No — NAS share settings require admin login. But with default credentials (admin/admin often unchanged), it's the classic \"post-auth RCE\" — still recordable as command injection (CWE-78), severity high, static-only (no dynamic verification possible: uclited loopback IPC; httpd trace under qemu fails).\n\nActually — hmm wait. Is there any unauthenticated path to trigger nasSambaSetShareList? tphotplug? No. It's admin-only.\n\nChain:\nPOST /userRpm/NasShareSettingsRpm.htm (admin auth) with shareName=`x;cmd;.smb.conf` → httpd swNas* → ucm IPC (127.0.0.1:51237) → uclited ucm_cfg_handler → nasSambaSetShareList(0x4a63ec) → snprintf(v6[128],\"rm -rf /tmp/samba/%s.smb.conf\", name) → system(v6) → RCE as root.\n\nWait, but there's an important detail: the injection point is inside the sub-share loop only when *(a1+68)!=0 (the intranet samba? \"LoadBalance\"?). The route: when a1+68==1 or 2 (samba service mode different from 0?). If a1+68==0, the share-list branch doesn't system() — it writes fprintf for each share (sprintf v5 into smb.conf). So system(v6) runs when *(a1+68) != 0 — i.e., when samba service type == 1/2 (probably \"samba\" vs \"ftp\"/\"both\"?). Hmm, a1+68 = samba enable/config-type. Actually 书: the code: `if (*(a1+68)) { fclose; if (*(a1+68)==2 || *(a1+68)==1) { v2=*(a1+76); if(v2) name=a1+80 else name=a1+256; snprintf...; system; ... } }`.\n\nSo the rm+system path needs samba enabled with a share \"(a1+76 hold?)\". Enough: precondition samba enabled + share name attacker-chosen via admin page.\n\nNow share name injection → is there any length/sanitization? snprintf bounded to v6[128] (0x80) — so injection payload must be ≤127 bytes total incl. prefix — plenty for `;cmd`.\n\nAlso secondary: sub_4A5EEC(a1, a1+176*v2+80) — builds share dir path... maybe also exec.\n\nThis is a solid finding: call_chain + poc. Let me also check the httpd endpoint that sets the share name: search \"NasShare\" functions in httpd.\n\nAlso check the second sprintf into v5 (smb.conf content) uses off_53F494 — writing share name into smb.conf (could contain newline → smb.conf directive injection, e.g., \"path = /\" + new","stream":true,"block":0}

