data: {"seq":1384,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:31:06.437Z","text":"uclited nasSambaSetShareList has in_degree 6 (6 call sites in uclited). Need to confirm where the share-name field (a1+80 / a1+256) gets filled with user input and, critically, whether it can contain shell metacharacters. The ucm \"nasSambaSetShareList\" operation is dispatched when httpd pushes NAS share config. The fields at a1+80 (64 bytes) / a1+256 are likely the \"share name\" / \"ftp share name\" (for \"samba+ftp\" combos: a1+76 = service index, 0=samba?1=ftp?2=both?). Actually the structure: 176-byte share-entry blocks; a1+80 is first share's name; a1+256 = second share's name (at 176+80=256). a1+76 = current share index v2; if v2 nonzero use a1+80 else a1+256? Look: `v2 = *(a1+76); if (v2) snprintf(..., (a1+80)); else snprintf(..., (a1+256));` — hmm a1+256 = a1+176+80 = offset of entry[1].name. So the rm -rf uses the name of share entry [v2] v2∈{0..} where entry0 name at a1+80, entry1 at a1+256, entry2 at 432. And the loop writes smb.conf via off_53F494 when a1+68==0.\n\nAnyway — reachability: which type of operation sets these? Not fully traceable purely static from here, but the field names and the ucm IPC are clear. This matches the real-world TP-Link vulnerability CVE-2020-8957? Actually the exact known bug: **CVE-2018-10201? no","stream":true,"block":0}

