data: {"seq":1395,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:31:09.885Z","text":"uclited nasSambaSetShareList has in_degree 6 (6 call sites in uclited). Need to confirm where the share-name field (a1+80 / a1+256) gets filled with user input and, critically, whether it can contain shell metacharacters. The ucm \"nasSambaSetShareList\" operation is dispatched when httpd pushes NAS share config. The fields at a1+80 (64 bytes) / a1+256 are likely the \"share name\" / \"ftp share name\" (for \"samba+ftp\" combos: a1+76 = service index, 0=samba?1=ftp?2=both?). Actually the structure: 176-byte share-entry blocks; a1+80 is first share's name; a1+256 = second share's name (at 176+80=256). a1+76 = current share index v2; if v2 nonzero use a1+80 else a1+256? Look: `v2 = *(a1+76); if (v2) snprintf(..., (a1+80)); else snprintf(..., (a1+256));` — hmm a1+256 = a1+176+80 = offset of entry[1].name. So the rm -rf uses the name of share entry [v2] v2∈{0..} where entry0 name at a1+80, entry1 at a1+256, entry2 at 432. And the loop writes smb.conf via off_53F494 when a1+68==0.\n\nAnyway — reachability: which type of operation sets these? Not fully traceable purely static from here, but the field names and the ucm IPC are clear. This matches the real-world TP-Link vulnerability CVE-2020-8957? Actually the exact known bug: **CVE-2018-10201? no.** There IS a documented TP-Link Archer family bug: \"NAS share name 命令注入\" via smb.conf: CVE-2020-... Let me not rely on CVE numbers.\n\nNow — one more idea for an UNAUTHENTICATED hit: what about the SMB share itself? The `smb.conf` via `fprintf(stream, v5)` uses off_53F494 — writing share name into smb.conf. If share NAME contains newline/directive (e.g. `%0a    path = /\\n    read only = no\\n    guest ok = yes`), attacker could inject directives — smb.conf injection (still post-auth).\n\nOK, so the honest reachability: admin-authenticated web → RCE. That's a real finding (command injection CWE-78, high severity in impact terms but gated by auth; TP-Link default creds make it widely reported).\n\nLet me also check the httpd side of nasSambaSetSysUser / nasSambaSetShareList to complete the chain entries: httpd nasSambaSetShareList (0x5463e0) — in_degree 7, and it probably receives from the NAS page handler that parses POST params (shareName etc). Let me read httpd 0x5463e0 to see if it copies httpGetEnv strings into dword_5D0950 struct (a1). That would complete: httpd reads \"shareName\" → builds struct → calls uclited? No — httpd's nasSambaSetShareList writes /tmp/samba/smb.conf ITSELF (fopen /tmp/samba/smb.conf \"w\" + sub_545E4C?? hmm `bt` shows fopen, sub_545E4C, sub_54610C, fclose, sub_545DA4, strcpy, dword_5D0950...). httpd also has system? bt doesn't show system in httpd version. Let me read httpd nasSambaSetShareList full — it may contain the same rm -rf system() (in httpd!).","stream":false,"block":0}

