data: {"seq":1448,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:31:31.916Z","text":"JACKPOT — nasSambaSetUserList (uclited 0x4a4b48):\n\n```c\nexecFormatCmd(\"%s -p %s -a %s\", \"smbpasswd\", v1, v2)\n```\nwhere:\n- v1 = &a1[44 * a1[1] + 6] — **user name?** offset 44*idx+6 → at 44*(index)+6 dwords? a1 is int*, so a1[44*i+6] = dword at byte offset 176*i+24. Hmm, wait — a1[44*i+2] appears as username (myStrlwr lowercases it); a1[44*i+6] = **password** (the passwd value!). Let me map to the nasSambaSetSysUser: `v2 = myStrlwr(&a1[44 * i + 2], v10); v3 = myStrlwr(&a1[44 * i + 2], v10); fprintf(stream, fmt, v2, v3)` — that writes smbpasswd-file entries using the SAME field a1[44i+2] twice (as login and as passwd?). In SetUserList: `v1 = &a1[44*a1[1]+6]` (different field, offset +6 dwords = password field?) and `v2 = myStrlwr(&a1[44*a1[1]+2], v6)` (username field). So execFormatCmd(\"smbpasswd -p <password> -a <username>\",) — wait: \"%s -p %s -a %s\" with args (\"smbpasswd\", v1, v2) → `smbpasswd -p","stream":true,"block":0}

