data: {"seq":1762,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:38:29.399Z","text":"sub_4255C8 (mode-2 session check):\n- If dword_5E69FC (LAN session flag): compares MAC/identity v4 (from sub_4254B8 - MAC from ARP for IP v1) with session_client (strncmp 0x12 bytes). So session is bound to the **client MAC address** (from ARP) + the session token in session_client. \n- Else (WAN login): checks source IP == dword_5E69DC.\n\nSo auth mode 2 = session tied to client MAC -> cookie + MAC. This is the classic TP-Link design. An attacker on the LAN knows... the session_client is derived from sub_425838 random token set at login (`strncpy(session_client, v6+4, 0x11)`). v6+4 = session object's MAC? sub_4252A8 gives session object keyed by client MAC. So mode 2 validates MAC","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":1763,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:38:29.757Z","text":"sub_4255C8 (mode-2 session check):\n- If dword_5E69FC (LAN session flag): compares MAC/identity v4 (from sub_4254B8 - MAC from ARP for IP v1) with session_client (strncmp 0x12 bytes). So session is bound to the **client MAC address** (from ARP) + the session token in session_client. \n- Else (WAN login): checks source IP == dword_5E69DC.\n\nSo auth mode 2 = session tied to client MAC -> cookie + MAC. This is the classic TP-Link design. An attacker on the LAN knows... the session_client is derived from sub_425838 random token set at login (`strncpy(session_client, v6+4, 0x11)`). v6+4 = session object's MAC? sub_4252A8 gives session object keyed by client MAC. So mode 2 validates MAC only?? It compares v4 (client MAC from ARP) with session_client (MAC stored at login). The cookie/token byte_5E69E8 isn't checked here?! That means **any LAN client with the same MAC as the PC that logged in passes** — i.e., once the admin logs in from PC X, ANY device spoofing PC X's MAC (or the same PC) passes without needing the Authorization cookie??","stream":true,"block":0}

