data: {"seq":1765,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:38:30.476Z","text":"sub_4255C8 (mode-2 session check):\n- If dword_5E69FC (LAN session flag): compares MAC/identity v4 (from sub_4254B8 - MAC from ARP for IP v1) with session_client (strncmp 0x12 bytes). So session is bound to the **client MAC address** (from ARP) + the session token in session_client. \n- Else (WAN login): checks source IP == dword_5E69DC.\n\nSo auth mode 2 = session tied to client MAC -> cookie + MAC. This is the classic TP-Link design. An attacker on the LAN knows... the session_client is derived from sub_425838 random token set at login (`strncpy(session_client, v6+4, 0x11)`). v6+4 = session object's MAC? sub_4252A8 gives session object keyed by client MAC. So mode 2 validates MAC only?? It compares v4 (client MAC from ARP) with session_client (MAC stored at login). The cookie/token byte_5E69E8 isn't checked here?! That means **any LAN client with the same MAC as the PC that logged in passes** — i.e., once the admin logs in from PC X, ANY device spoofing PC X's MAC (or the same PC) passes without needing the Authorization cookie?? Wait — where is the cookie checked? In sub_425AA4 mode1: `sub_425104` etc uses session from sub_4252A8 (keyed by IP/MAC?). Hmm, but the Authorization header is read in sub_425460 only to decide prompting. Actually maybe sub_4252A8 creates session keyed by source IP; the cookie is validated inside sub_425104 via sub_4250A0(a1) — let me check sub_4250A0. It probably hashes the Authorization header against the stored token.\n\nThis is the \"clickjacking/cookie-less\" potential area... Actually the notable weakness: **mode-2 auth on LAN = MAC binding, no cookie check** → session fixation? If sub_42595C path (mode 2) doesn't verify the cookie at all, then after login from any LAN host... hmm the \"session\" IS the client MAC. sub_4255C8: `strncmp(v4 /*client MAC*/, session_client, 0x12)` — session_client was set at login to the **MAC of","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":1766,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:38:30.837Z","text":"sub_4255C8 (mode-2 session check):\n- If dword_5E69FC (LAN session flag): compares MAC/identity v4 (from sub_4254B8 - MAC from ARP for IP v1) with session_client (strncmp 0x12 bytes). So session is bound to the **client MAC address** (from ARP) + the session token in session_client. \n- Else (WAN login): checks source IP == dword_5E69DC.\n\nSo auth mode 2 = session tied to client MAC -> cookie + MAC. This is the classic TP-Link design. An attacker on the LAN knows... the session_client is derived from sub_425838 random token set at login (`strncpy(session_client, v6+4, 0x11)`). v6+4 = session object's MAC? sub_4252A8 gives session object keyed by client MAC. So mode 2 validates MAC only?? It compares v4 (client MAC from ARP) with session_client (MAC stored at login). The cookie/token byte_5E69E8 isn't checked here?! That means **any LAN client with the same MAC as the PC that logged in passes** — i.e., once the admin logs in from PC X, ANY device spoofing PC X's MAC (or the same PC) passes without needing the Authorization cookie?? Wait — where is the cookie checked? In sub_425AA4 mode1: `sub_425104` etc uses session from sub_4252A8 (keyed by IP/MAC?). Hmm, but the Authorization header is read in sub_425460 only to decide prompting. Actually maybe sub_4252A8 creates session keyed by source IP; the cookie is validated inside sub_425104 via sub_4250A0(a1) — let me check sub_4250A0. It probably hashes the Authorization header against the stored token.\n\nThis is the \"clickjacking/cookie-less\" potential area... Actually the notable weakness: **mode-2 auth on LAN = MAC binding, no cookie check** → session fixation? If sub_42595C path (mode 2) doesn't verify the cookie at all, then after login from any LAN host... hmm the \"session\" IS the client MAC. sub_4255C8: `strncmp(v4 /*client MAC*/, session_client, 0x12)` — session_client was set at login to the **MAC of the login client**. So on LAN, EVERY request from that MAC is accepted — no cookie needed! That means after admin logs in once from a PC, the session persists for that MAC until... dword_5E69E0 (600 ticks timeout) or logoff. This is by-design \"remember session by MAC\". An attacker would need to spoof the MAC — plausible but not a clean vuln.\n\nThe clean finding here: **MAC-only session binding means no authentication token","stream":true,"block":0}

