that MAC is accepted — no cookie needed! That means after admin logs in once from a PC, the session persists for that MAC until... dword_5E69E0 (600 ticks timeout) or logoff. This is by-design \"remember session by MAC\". An attacker would need to spoof the MAC — plausible but not a clean vuln.\n\nThe clean finding here: **MAC-only session binding means no authentication token in requests; a LAN attacker who observes one valid session window can hijack by MAC spoofing** — that's more of a weakness than a hard vuln (requires same-AF spoofing which is normal within LAN). Not a solid recordable CVE without more.\n\nLet me check sub_4250A0 (auth header digest check) to see if mode1 also validates cookie... but mode1 is used when? Actually dword_5E69E4 is set to 2 after successful login (LoginRpmHtm case). So mode 2 = the actual post-login state! And mode 2's check (sub_42595C → sub_4255C8) does NOT look at the Authorization cookie at all — it checks MAC (LAN) or IP (WAN).\n\nHmm interesting but \"MAC spoofing\" is a weak vul","stream":true,"block":0}

