data: {"seq":1775,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:38:33.727Z","text":"sub_4255C8 (mode-2 session check):\n- If dword_5E69FC (LAN session flag): compares MAC/identity v4 (from sub_4254B8 - MAC from ARP for IP v1) with session_client (strncmp 0x12 bytes). So session is bound to the **client MAC address** (from ARP) + the session token in session_client. \n- Else (WAN login): checks source IP == dword_5E69DC.\n\nSo auth mode 2 = session tied to client MAC -> cookie + MAC. This is the classic TP-Link design. An attacker on the LAN knows... the session_client is derived from sub_425838 random token set at login (`strncpy(session_client, v6+4, 0x11)`). v6+4 = session object's MAC? sub_4252A8 gives session object keyed by client MAC. So mode 2 validates MAC only?? It compares v4 (client MAC from ARP) with session_client (MAC stored at login). The cookie/token byte_5E69E8 isn't checked here?! That means **any LAN client with the same MAC as the PC that logged in passes** — i.e., once the admin logs in from PC X, ANY device spoofing PC X's MAC (or the same PC) passes without needing the Authorization cookie?? Wait — where is the cookie checked? In sub_425AA4 mode1: `sub_425104` etc uses session from sub_4252A8 (keyed by IP/MAC?). Hmm, but the Authorization header is read in sub_425460 only to decide prompting. Actually maybe sub_4252A8 creates session keyed by source IP; the cookie is validated inside sub_425104 via sub_4250A0(a1) — let me check sub_4250A0. It probably hashes the Authorization header against the stored token.\n\nThis is the \"clickjacking/cookie-less\" potential area... Actually the notable weakness: **mode-2 auth on LAN = MAC binding, no cookie check** → session fixation? If sub_42595C path (mode 2) doesn't verify the cookie at all, then after login from any LAN host... hmm the \"session\" IS the client MAC. sub_4255C8: `strncmp(v4 /*client MAC*/, session_client, 0x12)` — session_client was set at login to the **MAC of the login client**. So on LAN, EVERY request from that MAC is accepted — no cookie needed! That means after admin logs in once from a PC, the session persists for that MAC until... dword_5E69E0 (600 ticks timeout) or logoff. This is by-design \"remember session by MAC\". An attacker would need to spoof the MAC — plausible but not a clean vuln.\n\nThe clean finding here: **MAC-only session binding means no authentication token in requests; a LAN attacker who observes one valid session window can hijack by MAC spoofing** — that's more of a weakness than a hard vuln (requires same-AF spoofing which is normal within LAN). Not a solid recordable CVE without more.\n\nLet me check sub_4250A0 (auth header digest check) to see if mode1 also validates cookie... but mode1 is used when? Actually dword_5E69E4 is set to 2 after successful login (LoginRpmHtm case). So mode 2 = the actual post-login state! And mode 2's check (sub_42595C → sub_4255C8) does NOT look at the Authorization cookie at all — it checks MAC (LAN) or IP (WAN).\n\nHmm interesting but \"MAC spoofing\" is a weak vuln. Let me confirm the cookie isn't checked elsewhere in the mode-2 path: sub_42595C: `if (!sub_4255C8(a1)) { dword_5A51C0=0; return 2; }` → if MAC matches → continue to `if status != 200...` → return 2 (allow handler run? or deny?).\n\nhttpDispatcher: `v15 = sub_425CB8(...); if (v15) return LoginRpmHtm`. Return 2 from sub_425CB8 → v15=2 → LoginRpmHtm. So return 2 = DENY (show login)?! Then in sub_42595C: MAC match → falls to `if (*(a1+48) != 200)` — status isn't 200 → v7 = sub_425460(a1) (auth header absent?) → ... return 2 → DENY. And if status==200 (login page?) & dword_5C1B30 logged-in... → return v5=2 → DENY?\n\nThis return-value interpretation is getting too risky. I need to stop guessing at framework semantics — misreading would produce a false finding. Focus instead on concrete, verifiable sinks.\n\nPlan for the rest of this round (concrete):\n1. Poll fz-cfcc95b7 (dhcp6s whole binary) until done.\n2. Wireless: complete the execWlanCmd caller chain. opWlan2G_fun_register registers module 25 with table dword_53D138 — the handler table. Let me look at what handler function receives the ucm \"Set\" op for wlan security and calls execWlanCmd — search uclited funcs with \"WlanConfigSet\"/\"WlanBasicConfigSet\" done earlier: ucWlanSecurityConfigSet just copies. The APPLY step: some function executes the WLAN commands after set: maybe \"ucWlanBasicConfigSet\" → calls \"swWlanApply\"→ hidden. Search pattern \"WlanApplyCmd\"? Let me search \"Apply\" in uclited.","stream":true,"block":0}

