data: {"seq":1985,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:40:19.298Z","text":"sub_49D184 (NasFolderSharingRpm.htm handler) — this is the page that handles:\n- accountSave → nas_admin_name / nas_admin_pwd → **strncpy((char*)&s[64], v7, 0x10u)** — NAS admin name 16 bytes; **strncpy((char*)&s[68], v8, 0x10u)** — admin pwd 16 bytes (both bounded). Then swNasSetSrvUserList(s).\n- subpage → **displayName** (share display name): `strcpy(v75, v18)` — v75 is `char v75[4]` !!! → **UNBOUNDED strcpy into 4-byte buffer** — stack overflow with displayName > 3 chars!!! Wait — v75[4] at sp+2C, then v76 at sp+30... `strcpy(v75, v18)` with v18 = displayName (unlimited attacker string via POST) → stack overflow!!! \n\nLet me double check: declarations:\n```c\nchar v75[4]; // [sp+2Ch] [-4174h] BYREF\nint v76; // [sp+30h] [-4170h]\n```\nAnd the code: \n```c\nv18 = (const char *)httpGetEnv(a1, \"displayName\");\nv19 = v18;\nif ( v18 )\n{\n  strcpy(v75, v18);\n  ...\n  strcpy((char *)&s[77 * v1 + 262], v75);   // stores display name as share's display name (16-byte field!)\n```\nHmm — but later `strncmp((const char *)&s[v21+262], v19, 0x10u)` compares display names to v19; and `strcpy(&s[77*v1+262], v75)` — the s[] field at +262 has 16-byte capacity (v75 as 4 bytes??). If displayName string is stored into s[77i+262] (16-byte field), a displayName > 16 chars would overflow s[]'s share entry (77 dwords = 308 bytes per entry, +262 dwords = +1048 bytes into entry0...). Wait offsets: s is _DWORD; &s[77*v1+262] = byte offset 4*(77*v1+262) = 308*v1 + 1048. Within a 308-byte entry (77 dwords), offset 1048?? That's beyond entry0 (0..307). Hmm s[260] is count; entries start at s+261 (1044). &s[77i+262] = 1048 + 308i. Entry fields: display name at +262d = 1048? The v75[4] buffer... this decompile has overlapping/odd offsets because s is _DWORD* and some indices are within-struct fields.\n\nThe clean fact: **strcpy(v75, v18)** where v75 is 4 bytes and v18 is the \"displayName\" POST param → **stack buffer overflow, unauthenticated? NO — after HttpAccessPermit only** — wait, the first lines: `if (!HttpAccessPermit(a1)) return HttpDenyPage(a1);` — HttpAccessPermit is the Host/Referer check only! No session check visible in this handler?! If HttpAccessPermit is ALL the auth this handler does, then... but httpDispatcher already ran sub_425CB8 which gates (for non-login handlers it calls sub_425CB8 before v16(a1)). sub_49D184 is registered as an RPM handler via httpRpmConfAdd → the dispatcher gates it with sub_425CB8 → requires valid session (mode2 → MAC/IP). So \"displayName\" overflow is authenticated (LAN MAC/IP-bound session, i.e., admin).\n\nStill — this is a REAL stack overflow: displayName POST param → strcpy into v75[4]. 4-byte buffer! Any displayName ≥ 4 chars overflows — NORMAL UI usage (a display name like \"MyShare\") triggers it! That's a *reliably triggerable* overflow (not requiring exotic input), post-auth. Same for the s[] writes.\n\nWait, v75[4] could be the decompiler showing the first 4 bytes of a larger buffer (like earlier v8[7]). The local list: v75[4] then v76,v77,v78 (ints) then v79[6], v80[8], src[36]... v75 is truly 4 bytes (sp+2C..2F), v76=sp+30. strcpy(v75, displayName-with-no-limit) writes from sp+2C upward: v76,v77,v78,v79,src,v82,v83,v84... up to v101 (sp+224, 256 bytes), v102, v103, v104[7792], s[1949] — the frame is huge (0x4188). strcpy dest sp+2C: a 20-char displayName writes 24 bytes → sp+2C..3F → corrupts v76..v79 region but still inside frame; a 2000-char displayName (HTTP env buffer allows?) → corrupts into s[] and eventually saved regs/RA. Post-auth DoS/RCE.\n\nBut hold on — is displayName length actually limited by the web UI? The JS limit, but handler doesn't enforce. POST directly with curl → full control.\n\nHmm, wait — actually check line: `strncpy(v101, v23, 0x100u)` for shareFolderName (folder path, bounded 256); displayName uses strcpy unbounded. Also `strcpy((char *)&s[77 * v1 + 262], v75)` — copies the (possibly overflowing) v75 into the share entry display-name field (&s[...+262]) which is followed by the folder-name field at +270 — strcpy writes displayName into a 32-byte (8 dword) region (262..269) then +270 is folder name — displayName >32 chars overflows INTO folder name field and beyond (entry is 77 dwords=308 bytes; +262..+269 = 8 dwords = 32 bytes; folder name at 270..? 270+ gets up to 304...). An unbounded strcpy at +262 overwrites the whole entry + next entries.\n\nSo TWO unbounded copies from displayName. This is a solid CWE-121 stack overflow finding (post-auth with HttpAccessPermit+session gate; real-world default-credential scenario).\n\nActually — WAIT. Critical: is sub_49D184 really only reachable logged-in? httpDispatcher: only pages registered as httpRpmFs or httpHelpRequestRpm bypass sub_425CB8. NasFolderSharingRpm is a regular RPM → gated by sub_425CB8. So auth required (but the auth = session cookie; default creds admin/admin).\n\nLet me also double check: before `if (httpGetEnv(a1,\"subpage\"))` — the displayName parsing is inside `if (httpGetEnv(a1, \"subpage\"))` block, requiring \"subpage\" param present. So PoC: POST /userRpm/NasFolderSharingRpm.htm with subpage=...&displayName=<long>.\n\nThis is worth recording as F3 (post-auth stack overflow; more reliable trigger than execWlanCmd key ones). Let me record it now, then poll fuzzes.\n\nseverity: high (authenticated RCE via stack overflow; no canary). confidence 0.45 static-only (decompiler buffer size uncertainty — v75[4] could be a bigger buffer; the s[] strcpy at +262 is independent and clearly unbounded though, and that one corrupts the heap/global? s[] is STACK (char s[1949*4] = 7796 bytes on stack!). strcpy into &s[77*v1+262] with unbounded displayName → s[] stack overflow too. Both copies unbounded.)\n\nEvidence: the handler strcpy(v75, v18); strcpy(&s[77*v1+262], v75) with v18=httpGetEnv(\"displayName\") unbounded; s[] is 7796-byte stack buffer. Frame has no canary (httpd checksec canary=false).\n\nNote: v75[4] might be 4 bytes real; even if decompiler splits, the second strcpy into stack s[] at fixed offset with attacker length > remaining space is conclusive overflow: field region 262..269 holds display name (32B), folder name at 270 (up to... following strncpy compares 0x100=256 for folder names at +270). So displayName beyond 32 bytes overflows the entry","stream":true,"block":0}

