e locals of a char* array? Actually v82[0]=(int)&s[19] etc are used as pageParaSet arrays — v75..v80, src, v82 are page-param descriptor arrays (each 8 bytes: ptr+len). v75 at sp+2C, v76 at sp+30: v75/v76 might form ONE 8-byte descriptor {ptr,len} — \"v75\" being the PTR field of a pageParam descriptor?! strcpy(v75, v18) writes the string into... the pointer field?! That would be writing the displayName CONTENT into a page-param descriptor's pointer slot — if it's really used as pointer later (`strcpy(&s[...], v75)` treats v75 as CHAR POINTER — it reads the string from v75 as pointer!). \n\nLook at usage: `strcpy((char *)&s[77 * v1 + 262], v75)` — v75 passed as char* SOURCE — meaning v75 holds a POINTER (address of the displayName string?) — but they `strcpy(v75, v18)` writing INTO v75 (as buffer)! Both uses: strcpy(dst=v75, src=v18) — writes string into v75-as-buffer; then strcpy(dst=&s[...], src=v75) — reads v75-as-string. So v75 IS a character buffer for displayName. Its decompiled size [4] plus next locals are the param-descriptor arrays... The descriptors v75/v76 (sp+2C..33) = 8 bytes → maybe v75 = 4-byte char buf?, no...\n\nWhatever the exact size, the SECOND strcpy's overflow needs 6.7KB — the FIRST needs just 4 bytes IF 4 bytes is right. Between uncertainty, my recorded finding claims both copies unbounded with v75[4] — phrased cautiously. Keep confidence 0.45.\n\nNow poll httpd fuzz.","stream":true,"block":0}

