data: {"seq":2154,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:46:01.170Z","text":"- httpd fuzz fz-7ea3a41f: **ok, 34,073 execs / 0 crash / 0.20% coverage** — matches orchestrator's number. Recorded.\n- vsftpd md5 = 78023622b84b6241bb5208a82785de7c (845 functions, IN-012, port 21, auth AS-AUTH-002).\n- dropbear & samba_multicall NOT decompiled in this job — no function source available. Can't do per-function static; but can fuzz whole-binary (fuzz doesn't need decompiled symbols; whole-binary mode just needs the md5 in manifest). Can I fuzz binaries not decompiled? binary_md5 must be in manifest — vsftpd/samba/dropbear md5s are in identification... For vsftpd md5 we have. samba_multicall md5 unknown (AS-015 didn't give md5). Try fuzz vsftpd whole binary (md5 78023622...) — vsftpd main will read /etc/vsftpd.conf; missing → error exit → forkserver should still engage like dhcp6s/httpd did (they got execs from startup path). Let's request vsftpd whole-binary fuzz.\n\nAlso try trace of vsftpd (port 21): qemu + vsftpd might actually listen (vsftpd single process, ftp_getline reads commands!) — vsftpd is a single-process server that could work under qemu-user (it forks per connection though; qemu linux-user supports fork). The earlier httpd/minidlna refused, but vsftpd might be more trace-friendly. Try: fw_request_trace vsftpd argv [] port 21 request","stream":true,"block":0}

