data: {"seq":2308,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:47:12.131Z","text":"vsftpd fuzz still running (~13 min). smbd not decompiled (no functions).\n\nLet me continue polling vsftpd fuzz. Also let me at least verify the vsftpd config-reading path doesn't have trivially exploitable issues in the TP-Link custom part: the parseconf functions are stock vsftpd (safe). The custom part: where does vsftpd read /tmp/vsftp/etc/vsftpd.conf? nasFtpCfgFileWrite writes it via fprintf with share paths... config injection (post-auth, authenticated access already gives root via smbpasswd injection) — skip.\n\nPoll vsftpd again in next call; space with","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":2309,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:47:12.486Z","text":"vsftpd fuzz still running (~13 min). smbd not decompiled (no functions).\n\nLet me continue polling vsftpd fuzz. Also let me at least verify the vsftpd config-reading path doesn't have trivially exploitable issues in the TP-Link custom part: the parseconf functions are stock vsftpd (safe). The custom part: where does vsftpd read /tmp/vsftp/etc/vsftpd.conf? nasFtpCfgFileWrite writes it via fprintf with share paths... config injection (post-auth, authenticated access already gives root via smbpasswd injection) — skip.\n\nPoll vsftpd again in next call; space with a different static: verify samba_multicall md5 via the ident doc earlier (IN-015 evidence: binary usr/sbin/samba_multicall present — no md5). Could get md5 via fw_get_identification full=1? The identification didn't include md5s. The surfaces have evidence with md5 for decompiled ones only. s","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":2310,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:47:12.838Z","text":"vsftpd fuzz still running (~13 min). smbd not decompiled (no functions).\n\nLet me continue polling vsftpd fuzz. Also let me at least verify the vsftpd config-reading path doesn't have trivially exploitable issues in the TP-Link custom part: the parseconf functions are stock vsftpd (safe). The custom part: where does vsftpd read /tmp/vsftp/etc/vsftpd.conf? nasFtpCfgFileWrite writes it via fprintf with share paths... config injection (post-auth, authenticated access already gives root via smbpasswd injection) — skip.\n\nPoll vsftpd again in next call; space with a different static: verify samba_multicall md5 via the ident doc earlier (IN-015 evidence: binary usr/sbin/samba_multicall present — no md5). Could get md5 via fw_get_identification full=1? The identification didn't include md5s. The surfaces have evidence with md5 for decompiled ones only. samba_multicall not decompiled → no md5 available → cannot fuzz it. dropbear likewise (not decompiled, AS-013 no md5). So vsftpd is the last fuzzable public service. OK.\n\nPoll vsftpd fuzz once more.","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":2311,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:47:13.271Z","text":"vsftpd fuzz still running (~13 min). smbd not decompiled (no functions).\n\nLet me continue polling vsftpd fuzz. Also let me at least verify the vsftpd config-reading path doesn't have trivially exploitable issues in the TP-Link custom part: the parseconf functions are stock vsftpd (safe). The custom part: where does vsftpd read /tmp/vsftp/etc/vsftpd.conf? nasFtpCfgFileWrite writes it via fprintf with share paths... config injection (post-auth, authenticated access already gives root via smbpasswd injection) — skip.\n\nPoll vsftpd again in next call; space with a different static: verify samba_multicall md5 via the ident doc earlier (IN-015 evidence: binary usr/sbin/samba_multicall present — no md5). Could get md5 via fw_get_identification full=1? The identification didn't include md5s. The surfaces have evidence with md5 for decompiled ones only. samba_multicall not decompiled → no md5 available → cannot fuzz it. dropbear likewise (not decompiled, AS-013 no md5). So vsftpd is the last fuzzable public service. OK.\n\nPoll vsftpd fuzz once more.","stream":false,"block":0}

