data: {"seq":2469,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:51:47.413Z","text":"Call trace API returns nothing useful again. OK, pivot: the login mechanism is understood well enough:\n- Basic auth + static pwd list (populated at runtime) + MAC/IP binding + URL token.\n- Default creds admin/admin on TP-Link — that's operational, not a vuln.\n\nGiven the login surface has no clean bypass found statically and I've spent many calls, I should now consider what NEW recordable findings remain. Options:\n\n1. **FTP vsftpd local-root via smbpasswd?** No.\n\n2. **The ucWlanCfgIsChanged (httpd 0x4bff00)** — not interesting.\n\n3. **A wild open NAS/Samba finding: smb.conf `guest ok` misconfig** — the nasSambaCfgFileWriteMainSec writes smb.conf; check whether guest access is enabled by default → unauth SMB access to /tmp/usbdisk → data exposure (IN-015 public!). The nasSambaCfgFileWriteMainSec function: fprintf lines: let me read it full (32 lines) — if it writes \"guest ok = yes\" or \"map to guest = Bad User\" always, then public SMB shares with read access = exposure finding (not RCE but a real vuln: CWE-284/306). Let me read nasSambaCfgFileWriteMainSec full.\n\n4. **vsftpd config: anon_enable?** nasFtpCfgFileWrite writes vsftpd.conf — if anonymous enabled → public FTP. The FTP users \"anon\"/\"guestanon\" with password = user-set — not anonymous. Config may set anonymous_enable=NO.\n\nLet me read nasSambaCfgFileWriteMainSec full and nasFtpCfgFileWrite full (51 lines) to check guest/anonymous exposure — this is a defensible IN-015/IN-012 exposure finding if misconfigured.","stream":true,"block":0}

