ose \"3200/3204/3208\" fields are uint16/DWORD mixes... Let me recompute: maybe the decompiler means (a1 + 524*i + 3200) where a1 is int = base, so byte offset = 4*(524i+3200). i=0 → 12800. Definitely beyond 6112. BUT perhaps the actual context: dns_decode_request is also called (in dns_read_packet) with a2 = packet buffer that is much bigger in OTHER callers? dns_read_packet is called from sub_401B38 with v6[1528], and possibly elsewhere with a different sized buffer. Only one caller in graph.\n\nSo dns_decode_request READS BEYOND v6 at dword offsets 3200.. for i<5 (up to ~21KB above the frame) and typically finds garbage/upstream fields — wait, actually could this be the answer-records region? qdcount i<5 loop uses 524*i+3200 as **dword** index — that would be for answers stored... answers parsed from upstream responses with their own slots at (262j+1316)*2=2632+524j bytes for uint16 indexing — i.e., BY","stream":true,"block":0}

