data: {"seq":2703,"ts":"2026-08-22T19:57:28.851Z","text":"**FOUND IT — dnsproxy name-decode overflow (CWE-121)**:\n\nsub_4028D8 (qname decoder — reachable from dns_read_packet→dns_decode_request→sub_402E18→sub_402A54→sub_4028D8 path, AND directly from dns_decode_request too):\n\n```c\nwhile (**a2) {                     // until zero label\n    v6 = *(*a2)++;                  // label length (attacker byte)\n    for (i = 0; i < v6; ++i) {\n        *(_BYTE *)(a1 + v7++) = **a2;   // <-- UNBOUNDED write into a1+v7\n        *a2 = v2 + 1;\n    }\n    *(_BYTE *)(a1 + v7++) = 46;     // '.'\n}\n*(_BYTE *)(a1 + v7 - 1) = **a2;    // overwrite trailing dot with 0\n```\n\n`a1 + v7` — where does a1 point and what's the buffer? Callers:\n1. From sub_402A54: `sub_4028D8(a1, &ptr)` — a1 = dest slot base = &a1[262i+6]... wait, in sub_402A54, the first arg is named a1 but it's the DEST slot passed from sub_402E18: `sub_402A54((int)&a1[262*i+6], a2, 1, v4, v3, a1)` — so in sub_402A54, its a1 = &outer_a1[262i+6] (dword index!) = byte offset (262i+6)*4. Hmm wait 262*i+6 in DWORDS = byte offset 1048*i+24. The qname decode writes into that slot...\n2. From dns_decode_request: `sub_4028D8(a1, &v6)` — a1 = the 6112-byte request buffer directly! So the decoder writes decoded qname starting at **offset 0 of the 6112-byte v6 buffer** — writing with v7 going unbounded up to the number of name bytes in a 512-byte packet!\n\n**Overflow analysis**: A DNS query packet (≤512 bytes, stored at a1+5584... wait no — in dns_decode_request, it parses the packet at (a1+6100) pointer... hold on: dns_decode_request: `*(a1+6100) = a1 + 5584; sub_402E18((uint16_t*)(a1+312), (uint16_t**)(a1+6100))` — sub_402E18's a2 = ptr to a1+6100 (pointer to packet at 5584), a1' (its first param) = &a1[312] — the parsed header slot. Then sub_402E18 calls sub_402A54(&a1[312+262i+6]... wait 262*i+6 added to a1' (which = outer a1+312) → destination slot at outer byte (312+262i+6)*4 = 1248 + 1048i. Hmm dword indexing: a1'[262i+6] = (262i+6)*4 bytes from a1' = 1248+1048i from outer a1.\n\nHmm wait — but actually ALL these are DWORDS: a1' is uint16_t* (2-byte type!) in sub_402E18 signature! `sub_402E18(uint16_t *a1, ...)` — a1[262i+6] in uint16_t units = byte 524i + 12 from a1'. And sub_402A54(int a1, ...) — first param as int addr = the ADDRESS: `sub_402A54((int)&a1[262*i+6], a2, 1, ...)` — &a1[262i+6] with a1=uint16_t* → byte offset (262i+6)*2 = 524i+12 from a1'... plus a1' = outer a1 + 312*2? No wait — a1' (first arg of sub_402E18) = (uint16_t*)(outer_a1 + 312)?? outer_a1 is int (byte addressing?): `sub_402E18((uint16_t *)(a1 + 312), (uint16_t **)(a1 + 6100))` — a1+312 as byte address → uint16_t*. So a1' = byte 312. Slots for questions at a1'[262i+6]*2 bytes = 312 + 524i + 12 = 324+524i. For i=0: byte 324 → qname decoded into dst at 324, with the packet at 5584. Qname slot is 1048*... unchecked write region? The slot a1+324.. and the decoded name length v7 — written to outer a1+324+v7 (via sub_4028D8(a1=dst?..)).\n\nWAIT — in sub_402A54, `sub_4028D8(a1, ...)` where a1 = the slot addr (324+524i). So decode writes INTO THE PACKET REGION?? a1=324, but the PACKET is at 5584 (a1+6100 pointer... no!). Hold on: sub_402A54's a1 = slot = 324.., and v4/v3/pkt params are the packet start (v4=v3=*a2 from sub_402E18 = the packet at 5584). So qname decode writes to offset 324 of the main buffer, packet resides at 5584..6095, no overlap for small names.\n\nThe qname bytes themselves: sub_4028D8 writes into `a1 + v7` where a1 = 324 (or 1316*2+312 for answers). v7 grows by label lengths. Max packet 512 bytes → max decoded name ~511 bytes → writes at 324..835 for qname. Then 524*4=2096 for 5 questions at most... For questions: slots at 324+524i, i<5 → last slot starts 324+2096=2420, +512 = 2932 < 5584 (packet) — safe within buffer. For answers: slots at a1'[262j+1316]*2 = 312+524j+2632 = 2944+524j; j=4 → 5040; +512 → 5552 < 5584!! close but just under the packet start. Hmm — AND dns_decode_request ALSO decodes the answer NAME into the SAME a1+255 area from packet...\n\nBut wait — the critical thing: sub_4028D8's writes are bounded only by the input name length (v6 = per-label byte ≤ 63, total ≤ 511 in a 512-byte packet) BUT NOT bounded against the DESTINATION slot size! With qdcount up to 5 AND arcount etc, plus dns_decode_request's copy of (a1+324) into a1... Safe-ish for the 6112-byte stack buffer? The decode writes are into fixed slots separated by 524 bytes; a qname ≤512 bytes fits within a slot (524) — actually if a qname is longer than 524 - 12 = 512... names are ≤ ~255 bytes normally, and even a pathological 511-byte name overflows the 524-slot into the next slot region, but stays within the 6112-byte buffer. Within-struct corruption possible but not crossing the stack frame unless we exceed total buffer (~5552 max end < 6112). Bounded overall by construction.\n\nHmm wait — for ANSWERS (j loop) the dest = 2944+524j; the ANSWER name parse happens against the UPSTREAM response (dns_output_sock recv, ≤512)... The packets come from two sockets:\n- dns_sock: LAN queries (attacker directly!)\n- dns_output_sock: upstream responses (attacker if they control upstream DNS or spoof it!)\n\nsub_401B38: for dns_sock → dns_read_packet → dns_decode_request; for dns_output_sock → dns_read_packet → dns_probe_response... wait: outputsock → dns_read_packet(dns_output_sock, v6) && dns_probe_response. And dns_read_packet calls dns_decode_request for both! So the parse of LAN queries at 5584 with slots at 324+524i — a query packet from the LAN with qdcount=5 (max) and crafted names → writes up to 5552 — within buffer.\n\n**The dangerous write in dns_decode_request**: `strncpy((char *)(a1 + 255), (const char *)(a1 + 324), 0x2E)` — bounded 46. And `if qtype==1||28: strncpy((char*)a1, (const char*)(a1+324), 0xFF)` — bounded 255. And `dns_decode_request`'s loop uses a1+524*i+3200/3204/3208 (dword index = byte 12800i?? no — a1 is int, 524*i+3200 in DWORD = byte 2096i+12800 — beyond 6112!). Wait: `*(_DWORD *)(a1 + 524 * i + 3200)` — this indexes a1 as int* → dword offset (524i+3200) → byte offset (524i+3200)*4 = 2096i + 12800. For i=0: 12800 > 6112!! **OUT OF THE v6 BUFFER** — reading beyond the 6112-byte stack buffer!\n\nHmm, that can't be right — v6 is 1528 dwords = 6112 bytes; offset 12800 is beyond. Unless the recvfrom buffer is actually part of a LARGER struct... In sub_401B38: `_DWORD v6[1528]; [sp+48h]` then memset(v6,0,0x17DC) = 6108 bytes. So v6 = 6112 bytes. dns_decode_request reads `*(_DWORD*)(a1 + 3204)` etc for i in 0..4 → offsets 12800..21044 — way beyond v6!! These would read into the caller's stack frame (sub_401B38's frame + beyond — even past the saved ra). Actually wait — a1 here is passed as `(int)v6` — same address. Reading offset 12800 of a 6112-byte buffer = reading the STACK BEYOND the frame — garbage / adjacent data, and `inet_ntop(2, &v5, (char*)(a1+255), 0x2E)` writes 46 bytes at 255 — safe write; the reads are the corrupted behavior (info from stack). Since v6 was memset to zero only for 0x17DC (6108) bytes... hmm 0x17DC = 6108; used loop `memset(v6, 0, 0x17DC)` where v6 is _DWORD — memset takes bytes: 0x17DC bytes = 6108 — ok within.\n\nHmm wait, that reading beyond doesn't match \"struct with answers at dword 3200\". Actually maybe a1 is NOT v6 base... in sub_401B38: `dns_read_packet(dns_sock, v6)` → a2 = v6 → `a2[1524] = recvfrom(a1, a2 + 1396, ...)` (dwords: 1396*4 = 5584 ✓). dns_decode_request(a2=v6). So yes a1 = v6 base. And `*(_DWORD*)(a1 + 524*i + 3200)` is dword index 3200+524i → byte 12800+... **reads beyond the buffer**. BUT — the *(_WORD*)(a1+580) reads dword index 145 → byte 580 ✓. So this struct uses DIFFERENT element types?! a1+580 as WORD index? `*(_WORD*)(a1+580)` byte offset 580. And `*(a1+318)` = dword? `v2 = *(unsigned __int16 *)(a1 + 318)` — byte 318. And `*(_DWORD*)(a1 + 524*i + 3200)` — with a1 as int* in the expression a1+524*i+3200 → scaled by 4. 3200*4=12800.\n\nThat's inconsistent — unless those \"3200/3204/3208\" fields are uint16/DWORD mixes... Let me recompute: maybe the decompiler means (a1 + 524*i + 3200) where a1 is int = base, so byte offset = 4*(524i+3200). i=0 → 12800. Definitely beyond 6112. BUT perhaps the actual context: dns_decode_request is also called (in dns_read_packet) with a2 = packet buffer that is much bigger in OTHER callers? dns_read_packet is called from sub_401B38 with v6[1528], and possibly elsewhere with a different sized buffer. Only one caller in graph.\n\nSo dns_decode_request READS BEYOND v6 at dword offsets 3200.. for i<5 (up to ~21KB above the frame) and typically finds garbage/upstream fields — wait, actually could this be the answer-records region? qdcount i<5 loop uses 524*i+3200 as **dword** index — that would be for answers stored... answers parsed from upstream responses with their own slots at (262j+1316)*2=2632+524j bytes for uint16 indexing — i.e., BYTE offsets 2632+524j — NOT 12800. \n\nSo `*(_DWORD *)(a1 + 524 * i + 3200)` with byte offset 4*(524i+3200)... conflicts. UNLESS a1 is `int*`-typed and the +524*i+3200 arithmetic was on a DIFFERENT pointer type originally (uint16). If it's uint16 indexing: byte = 2*(524i+3200) = 1048i+6400 → i=0: 6400 > 6112?! still beyond. If uint8: 524i+3200 → 3200..5296 → WITHIN the buffer (packet at 5584..6095; 3200..5296 is before packet — the \"decoded answer\" area!).\n\nLook at usage: `*(_DWORD *)(a1 + 304) = *(_DWORD *)(a1 + 524 * i + 3204)` — 304 is byte. And `*(unsigned __int16 *)(a1 + 524 * i + 3200)` for qtype compare with a1+580. So 3200-ish offsets as BYTE addresses: 3200+524i byte offsets — within the 6112 buffer (i=0..5: 3200..5820 — 5820 > 5584 overlaps packet start!). Hmm 3200..5820 for i≤5 — the region 3200..5584 (non-packet data) + 5584..5820 (packet start).\n\nSo if the compiler treats a1 as a byte-address offset base (i.e., the decompiler's \"a1 + 524*i + 3200\" where a1 is the buffer) — these are byte offsets. I originally mis-scaled. Given the mixture of *(a1+580) etc all byte offsets — YES, all these are BYTE offsets! The decompiler types the buffer as int* but uses raw byte offsets. So:\n- answers region at byte 3200+524i, qtype WORD at 3200, addr at 3208+... matches `(a1 + 524*i + 3208 + 2)` passed to inet_ntop — byte 3210+524i for A records. \n\nThat resolves it — everything within 6112 bytes. So the qname decode into 324+524i slots (<524 bytes per label sequence) — bounded within the buffer, with adjacent-slot overwrite possible on >524-byte names (not feasible: a single qname within a 512-byte packet maxes ~511 bytes with many labels of 63+... a 511-byte name would overflow slot (524) barely into next slot — but the name is in the question; qdcount loop bounds to min(qdcount,5)). Since ALL vars are in one 6112-byte stack frame, worst-case overflow stays inside the frame locals... UNLESS the buffer passed is smallER somewhere — dns_probe_response path etc.\n\nEnough: dnsproxy's decode is bounded by construction (512-byte packets, 524-byte slots, single 6112-byte stack buffer). The `v7-1` indexing:\n```c\n*(_BYTE *)(a1 + v7 - 1) = **a2;\n```\nwhen the FIRST byte is 0 (empty name) → v7=0 → writes at a1-1 (ONE BYTE BEFORE THE BUFFER)! A DNS query with an EMPTY qname (first byte 0x00 = root domain query \".\") → sub_4028D8: while(**a2) skipped (first byte 0) → v3=*a2; `*(a1 - 1) = **a2` (= 0) → **1-byte UNDERFLOW write before the buffer** — inside the caller's frame (v6 is preceded by timeout struct field at sp+40..; v6 at sp+48). Writing v6[-1] = sp+47 — one byte into the timeout/other local — minor intra-frame corruption. For LAN queries, qname empty = \".\" query — legit DNS! So every \".\" (root) query causes a 1-byte underflow — low impact but a real off-by-one bug. Given other locals nearby, likely harmless. Not a strong finding.\n\nOverall dnsproxy looks mostly bounded. The strongest remaining claim would be the a1-1 underflow — too weak.\n\nI've spent enough; the dnsproxy surface analysis concludes as \"bounded, minor underflow\". Let me finalize this round's status with: vsftpd fuzz closed (46,652), dnsproxy swept (no high-value finding), and give the orchestrator a crisp summary with pending items. I will NOT claim full completion.","stream":true,"block":0}

