data: {"seq":2822,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:02:16.145Z","text":"sub_400F68 (athadhoc):\n- dest is a char[4] but the code treats dest..v19 as a 32-byte zeroed struct, then `strncpy(dest, src, 0x10)` — copies 16 bytes of src into the 32-byte region dest[0..15] → **writes v13, v14, v15, v16 (first 4 bytes)... up to 16 bytes total — within the 32-byte region!** dest[4]+v13+v14+v15 = 16 bytes; strncpy writes exactly 16 bytes → fills dest..v15 (16 bytes) — **stays INSIDE the 32-byte region** (dest..v19 = 32 bytes). So the strncpy is NOT an overflow — dest is the *first 4 bytes* of a 32-byte union/struct; the decompiler only sized dest[4] but v13-v19 are the remainder. strncpy(16) covers dest+v13+v14+v15. Safe.\n- `memcpy(&v16, a3, n)` for n<16 — v16+v17+v18+v19 = 16 bytes region; n<16 → safe.","stream":true,"block":0}

