data: {"seq":11,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:11:20.148Z","text":"The orchestrator provides md5s now:\n- dropbear = scp md5 = 831411ed7e1770609b0ab9837d576e2f (decompiled now!)\n- smbd = samba_multicall md5 = 8de9773f9eb69f9ed433c6a50d053ea2\n\nAlso: non-HTTP ports don't need request_path=/ — omit request_path to trigger TCP/UDP probe.\n\nNew plan:\n1. Trace dropbear (port 22) with binary_md5 831411ed..., argv [], omit request_path (TCP probe).\n2. Trace smbd (port 445) with md5 8de9773f..., omit request_path.\n3. Whole-binary fuzz dropbear and smbd (md5s given).\n4. Static: dropbear 831411ed — now decompiled; mine dropbear for parsing issues (ssh handshake KEX buffering etc.)? Dropbear is upstream; check version-specific vulns. But at least scan for obvious memcpy/sprintf issues in auth/kex code.\n5. samba_multicall 8de9773f — check smbd entry: it's a multicall; whole-binary fuzz would run smbd (argv[0] smbd?). In fuzz mode argv can't be set (whole-binary mode has no argv param — the tool runs binary without args? args param is for function mode arg spec... For whole-binary mode, args is null. The binary name determines applet? samba_multicall dispatched by argv[0] — if run as \"samba_multicall\" with no argv0, its main dispatches based on argv[0] basename — \"samba_multicall\" unknown → maybe defaults to smbd or prints usage. Fuzz whole-binary then exercises whatever main does.\n\nLet me start: request trace dropbear:22, trace smbd:445 (no request_path), fuzz dropbear whole binary, fuzz smbd whole binary. That's 4 requests; fuzz quota unknown (used 6 plus some earlier; may hit limit). Do traces first (2 trace slots?) then fuzz (2).\n\nActually — do them in parallel batches: traces (2) + fuzz (2).","stream":true,"block":0}

