data: {"seq":45,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:11:39.109Z","text":"The dropbear in this build: 0x418e58 read_packet, common_recv_msg_channel_data at 0x40cb90 (cbuf I/O). This looks like **dropbear 0.5x-2013 era** (cbuf-based write). Known vuln for this era: **CVE-2016-7407/7408/7409 (dropbear 2016)**, **CVE-2013-4421**... Can't pin without version strings. The read_packet function (0x418e58) in OLD dropbear (≤0.53) had a buffer overflow via packet length (fixed in 0.53: \"read_packet() could overflow if packet length > MAX_PACKET_LEN","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":46,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:11:39.461Z","text":"The dropbear in this build: 0x418e58 read_packet, common_recv_msg_channel_data at 0x40cb90 (cbuf I/O). This looks like **dropbear 0.5x-2013 era** (cbuf-based write). Known vuln for this era: **CVE-2016-7407/7408/7409 (dropbear 2016)**, **CVE-2013-4421**... Can't pin without version strings. The read_packet function (0x418e58) in OLD dropbear (≤0.53) had a buffer overflow via packet length (fixed in 0.53: \"read_packet() could overflow if packet length > MAX_PACKET_LEN\"). Let me read 0x418e58 (dropbear read_packet) — if it lacks the `if (len > MAX_PACKET_LEN) dropbear_exit` check → we found the classic vuln. This is worth verifying directly.","stream":true,"block":0}

