data: {"seq":75,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:11:50.130Z","text":"Trace results:\n- **dropbear:22 trace bf7475e2ec87: FAILED** — \"failed to bind host port 127.0.0.1:22/tcp: address already in use\" — the host's port 22 is occupied (real sshd on the host!). Infrastructure collision, not a firmware limitation. Retry would hit same. Note it.\n- **smbd:445 trace 8af796f4fe21: ok_empty_diff** — TCP connect probe succeeded to 445 (samba_multicall bound and accepted the connect under qemu!). 13 baseline functions; trigger tcp_connect completed (status ok, no error!). diff 0 — connect-only doesn't exercise SMB parsing, but this is the first trace where the trigger itself did NOT error (ok_empty_diff, not ok_empty_diff_trigger_error!). Documented: samba_multicall starts and listens on 445 under qemu. \n\nInteresting: smbd listen works under qemu — but 13 functions suggests it bound quickly. A real SMB handshake would need an SMB client; the tracer only does TCP connect. Fine — evidence of listener.\n\nFuzzes: both running.\n\nGiven smbd runs under qemu, the whole-binary fuzz of samba_multicall might actually progress (it will spawn... it's a multicall; running bare \"samba_multicall\" argv[0]→ main dispatches unknown applet → probably usage/exit → AFL counts startup execs). \n\nNow, while fuzzes run, mine smbd config/parse: smbd reads /etc/samba/smb.conf (per earlier","stream":true,"block":0}

