data: {"seq":76,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:11:50.552Z","text":"Trace results:\n- **dropbear:22 trace bf7475e2ec87: FAILED** — \"failed to bind host port 127.0.0.1:22/tcp: address already in use\" — the host's port 22 is occupied (real sshd on the host!). Infrastructure collision, not a firmware limitation. Retry would hit same. Note it.\n- **smbd:445 trace 8af796f4fe21: ok_empty_diff** — TCP connect probe succeeded to 445 (samba_multicall bound and accepted the connect under qemu!). 13 baseline functions; trigger tcp_connect completed (status ok, no error!). diff 0 — connect-only doesn't exercise SMB parsing, but this is the first trace where the trigger itself did NOT error (ok_empty_diff, not ok_empty_diff_trigger_error!). Documented: samba_multicall starts and listens on 445 under qemu. \n\nInteresting: smbd listen works under qemu — but 13 functions suggests it bound quickly. A real SMB handshake would need an SMB client; the tracer only does TCP connect. Fine — evidence of listener.\n\nFuzzes: both running.\n\nGiven smbd runs under qemu, the whole-binary fuzz of samba_multicall might actually progress (it will spawn... it's a multicall; running bare \"samba_multicall\" argv[0]→ main dispatches unknown applet → probably usage/exit → AFL counts startup execs). \n\nNow, while fuzzes run, mine smbd config/parse: smbd reads /etc/samba/smb.conf (per earlier nasSambaCfgFileWriteMainSec writes /tmp/samba/smb.conf). smbd invoked as \"smbd -D\" reads /etc/samba/smb.conf — not present → may exit. Whole-binary fuzz: samba_multicall with argv[0]=\"samba_multicall\" → its main (multicall) switches on argv[0] basename: \"samba_multicall\" → probably prints usage and exits → 50k startup","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":77,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:11:50.903Z","text":"Trace results:\n- **dropbear:22 trace bf7475e2ec87: FAILED** — \"failed to bind host port 127.0.0.1:22/tcp: address already in use\" — the host's port 22 is occupied (real sshd on the host!). Infrastructure collision, not a firmware limitation. Retry would hit same. Note it.\n- **smbd:445 trace 8af796f4fe21: ok_empty_diff** — TCP connect probe succeeded to 445 (samba_multicall bound and accepted the connect under qemu!). 13 baseline functions; trigger tcp_connect completed (status ok, no error!). diff 0 — connect-only doesn't exercise SMB parsing, but this is the first trace where the trigger itself did NOT error (ok_empty_diff, not ok_empty_diff_trigger_error!). Documented: samba_multicall starts and listens on 445 under qemu. \n\nInteresting: smbd listen works under qemu — but 13 functions suggests it bound quickly. A real SMB handshake would need an SMB client; the tracer only does TCP connect. Fine — evidence of listener.\n\nFuzzes: both running.\n\nGiven smbd runs under qemu, the whole-binary fuzz of samba_multicall might actually progress (it will spawn... it's a multicall; running bare \"samba_multicall\" argv[0]→ main dispatches unknown applet → probably usage/exit → AFL counts startup execs). \n\nNow, while fuzzes run, mine smbd config/parse: smbd reads /etc/samba/smb.conf (per earlier nasSambaCfgFileWriteMainSec writes /tmp/samba/smb.conf). smbd invoked as \"smbd -D\" reads /etc/samba/smb.conf — not present → may exit. Whole-binary fuzz: samba_multicall with argv[0]=\"samba_multicall\" → its main (multicall) switches on argv[0] basename: \"samba_multicall\" → probably prints usage and exits → 50k startup exec again. OK.\n\nStatic","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":78,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:11:53.258Z","text":"Trace results:\n- **dropbear:22 trace bf7475e2ec87: FAILED** — \"failed to bind host port 127.0.0.1:22/tcp: address already in use\" — the host's port 22 is occupied (real sshd on the host!). Infrastructure collision, not a firmware limitation. Retry would hit same. Note it.\n- **smbd:445 trace 8af796f4fe21: ok_empty_diff** — TCP connect probe succeeded to 445 (samba_multicall bound and accepted the connect under qemu!). 13 baseline functions; trigger tcp_connect completed (status ok, no error!). diff 0 — connect-only doesn't exercise SMB parsing, but this is the first trace where the trigger itself did NOT error (ok_empty_diff, not ok_empty_diff_trigger_error!). Documented: samba_multicall starts and listens on 445 under qemu. \n\nInteresting: smbd listen works under qemu — but 13 functions suggests it bound quickly. A real SMB handshake would need an SMB client; the tracer only does TCP connect. Fine — evidence of listener.\n\nFuzzes: both running.\n\nGiven smbd runs under qemu, the whole-binary fuzz of samba_multicall might actually progress (it will spawn... it's a multicall; running bare \"samba_multicall\" argv[0]→ main dispatches unknown applet → probably usage/exit → AFL counts startup execs). \n\nNow, while fuzzes run, mine smbd config/parse: smbd reads /etc/samba/smb.conf (per earlier nasSambaCfgFileWriteMainSec writes /tmp/samba/smb.conf). smbd invoked as \"smbd -D\" reads /etc/samba/smb.conf — not present → may exit. Whole-binary fuzz: samba_multicall with argv[0]=\"samba_multicall\" → its main (multicall) switches on argv[0] basename: \"samba_multicall\" → probably prints usage and exits → 50k startup exec again. OK.\n\nStatic on smbd: it's the same md5 as samba_multicall — huge, not decompiled per surface; but md5 given says \"smbd is samba_multicall 符号链接\" — the module may be partially decompiled under samba_multicall name? Search for samba_multicall module functions: earlier \"samba_multicall\" search gave only routes. Not decompiled. So static mining not possible; fuzz only.\n\nLet me poll fuzzes; interleave with checking dropbear more: dropbear is the one decompiled (scp_831411ed module has dropbear_* functions). Check svr session main handlers for obvious issues? Standard dropbear. The likely real attack on Archer C7 dropbear: weak default hostkey? Not рекордable.\n\nRound out: poll fz-a1b8dbd9 & fz-78faacd0.","stream":false,"block":0}

