data: {"seq":291,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:14:07.190Z","text":"sub_49E330 (NasFolderAdvRpmNew):\n- v90[256]: `strcpy(v90, \"/tmp/usbdisk/\"); strcat(v90, v21)` — v21 = httpGetEnv(\"volumnName\"). v90 is 256 bytes; prefix 13 bytes → overflow needs volumnName ≥ 242 chars. Volume names are short (e.g., \"volume1\" from mount). POST-controlled though — a 250-char volumnName would overflow v90[256] → stack overflow. But is 256-byte volumnName realistic? Web UI volume","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":292,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:14:07.544Z","text":"sub_49E330 (NasFolderAdvRpmNew):\n- v90[256]: `strcpy(v90, \"/tmp/usbdisk/\"); strcat(v90, v21)` — v21 = httpGetEnv(\"volumnName\"). v90 is 256 bytes; prefix 13 bytes → overflow needs volumnName ≥ 242 chars. Volume names are short (e.g., \"volume1\" from mount). POST-controlled though — a 250-char volumnName would overflow v90[256] → stack overflow. But is 256-byte volumnName realistic? Web UI volume select is a fixed list — but direct POST with volumnName=250 chars works. Same class as displayName (post-auth, direct POST). This IS another stack overflow instance in the NAS family — same theme; arguably worth including as part of the same finding cluster, but it's a separate handler","stream":true,"block":0}

