data: {"seq":8,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:17:37.066Z","text":"The orchestrator repeats the same guidance. First: poll fz-78faacd0 once more (it's the only running fuzz; maybe it completed by now).\n\nThen continue on the \"未扫完\" — dropbear (decompiled, 831411ed) deserves deeper static mining now that it's decompiled: the SSH KEX/auth paths. Let me look at dropbear's packet processing chain for genuinely NEW findings:\n- svr_auth_password / button... the auth checks against /etc/passwd? TP-Link dropbear allows root login with... The admin password on the router is stored in nvram and /etc/passwd; dropbear auth via PAM? uClibc — dropbear uses /etc/passwd getpwnam. If the passwd has no shell for root? whatever.\n- More valuable: dropbear's **session key / algorithm negotiation parsing** — crypto code from that era had issues, but standard.\n\nActually — one specific real-world TP-Link issue: **dropbear host key generation at first boot** — if /etc/dropbear/ doesn't exist, dropbear generates keys at boot — not a vuln.\n\nLet me instead check dropbear \"svr_getopts\" for the option string and how it reads /etc/dropbear options — no.\n\nBetter: since dropbear md5 == scp md5, this binary also contains **scp client + sftp-server** — the spawn candidates /bin/sh etc. scp client parsing of remote filenames → not server-side.\n\nGiven the instruction, the pragmatic task: keep polling fz-78faacd0, and re-attempt the dropbear trace on port 22 (maybe host freed; likely same failure), plus deeper static on dropbear packet handling: buf","stream":true,"block":0}

