data: {"seq":97,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:18:44.329Z","text":"**svr_chk_pwd — TP-Link dropbear 定制认证**:\n\n```c\nsvr_chk_pwd(const char *a1 /*username*/, int a2 /*fname*/)\n- fopen64(a2, \"r\") — opens a config FILE (path passed as a2; likely \"/var/...\" or /tmp/... of TP-Link's login config!)\n- parses lines \"username:xxx\" / \"password:xxx\" into v16[4+...] (username 16B at v16) and s[72] (password, 32B)\n- gets buf_getbool → if true: auth failure (probably \"Account disabled\" flag)\n- else: gets user-supplied password (client pwd) via buf_getstring (SSH auth payload)\n- verifies: username matches v16 AND **hex_md5_verify_digest(s, v10, v12)** — the stored \"password:xxx\" value is the header of MD5? — `hex_md5_verify_digest(s, supplied, len)` verifies the client-supplied password's MD5 hex against s!\n\nSo the FTP-style /tmp/... account file: `username:...\\npassword:<hex md5 digest>` — auth = compare MD5(clientpwd) to stored hex. The FILE (a2) — from svr_getopts / the auth code — path probably \"/var/tmp/... or /etc/...\". And v16/s limited copies (strncpy 16/32) — bounded.\n\nIs there a vuln? Key observations:\n1. **`v16[strlen(v9)] = 0`** — if v9 (password line value) length ≥ 16 (strncpy 16), strlen(v9) > 15 → writes s[16? no — v16[4 bytes] + v17+v18+v19+v20 = 17 bytes region; strncpy(v16, v9, 0x10) fills 16 bytes (v16..v19), then `v16[strlen(v9)]=0` — with strlen(v9) up to... s is set by strncpy(dest=s, v9, 0x20) in the other branch — same v9 (password value). If strlen(v9)=20 → v16[20]=0 writes byte at v16+20 = v20+3 (v20 at sp+3C, offsets: v16[0..3]=sp+2C..2F, v17=30..33, v18=34..37, v19=38..3B, v20=3C). v16[20] = sp+2C+20 = sp+40 = **s[0]** (s at sp+40)! Each line's \"username:...\" value sets NUL at s[strlen-16]... messy but writes within frame; v16/region 17 bytes but index 20 → off-by-3 into s[0..2]. It's a 1-3 byte overrun within frame — weak.\n\n2. **The critical part: auth is entirely based on a FILE the router writes** — where does that file come from and who can influence it? If a2 (~/etc/ or /tmp passwd file for SSH) is WRITABLE by an unprivileged process or contains the SAME password as the HTTP admin password from nvram... Also the TP-L","stream":true,"block":0}

