data: {"seq":173,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:21:07.766Z","text":"The orchestrator keeps looping with the same message. All fuzzes closed, all static surfaces covered. The genuinely remaining items are tool-blocked. But I should keep making real attempts in new directions, not just repeat.\n\nNew angles I haven't tried:\n1. **minidlna's libavformat/libavcodec usage** — the \"ff_\" paths in libavformat (01312699) were P1-scored (ff_udp_set_remote_url, ff_rtsp_connect strcpy!). Wait — those were in the top attack surface list with score 6.05! I dismissed them (\"RTSP only from config\"). But let me reconsider: minidlna uses libavformat to probe media files on the NAS — if the SMB/path name of a media FILE contains a \"rtsp://\" prefix or a crafted URL handled by avformat_open_input with a special filename like \"http://attacker/...\" — minidlna opens media files by path from the DB; the filenames come from disk (attacker could put a file named \"rtsp://evil/\" on the USB drive!) → ff_rtsp_connect with attacker-controlled URL → the strcpy overflow in ff_rtsp_connect (0x6d968: strcpy(dest, &s[1601])). Hmm — but avformat_open_input only treats rtsp:// as RTSP if the protocol is registered & the filename matches. A NAS file named \"rtsp://...\" is unlikely to be opened via the rtsp demuxer (probe order: file probing by content first?). Actually avformat_open_input checks filename prefixes first (\"rtsp://\" → rtsp demuxer). So a file named `<rtsp://evil>:...` WOULD be parsed","stream":true,"block":0}

