data: {"seq":217,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:21:23.490Z","text":"ff_udp_set_remote_url: `memcpy(v4+28, v5->ai_addr, v5->ai_addrlen)` — the target (v4+28) is a sockaddr inside a URL context struct; ai_addrlen for IPv6 = 28 bytes → writes 28 bytes to a region that might be sized for IPv4 sockaddr (16 bytes)? The struct (UDPContext) at v4: sockaddr_storage is usually sized for IPv6 (28). In FFmpeg's udp.c, `s->dest_addr` is `struct sockaddr_storage` (128 bytes) — so +28 ok. ai_addrlen = 28 (v6->ai_addrlen stored at +156 = address length field = 28; the socket connect uses it). No overflow (sockaddr_storage is big). Non-issue — FFmpeg stock code. The AI review's \"memcpy may overflow\" is a false positive given sockaddr_storage. Not recordable.\n\nff_rtsp_connect: the flagged line: `strcpy(dest[64], (const char *)&s[1601])` — s is _DWORD s[1716] = 6864 bytes. s[1601] = byte offset 6404 of the RESPONSE struct (the OPTIONS reply parsed into s[]). dest is 64 bytes. strcpy source = response field at s[1601] — content from the RTSP server reply (\"public:\" method list in the OPTIONS response: the \"Public:\" header value!). An attacker-controlled RTSP server response \"Public:\" header can be > 64 bytes → **stack overflow of dest[64]**. BUT — the trigger requires connecting to an attacker RTSP URL, which (a) comes from minidlna opening a media filename starting with \"rtsp://\" (attacker places a file with such a name on the USB/network share), or (b) RTSP server URL set in config. Also — request: ff_rtsp_connect runs when avformat opens rtsp:// — the response comes from attacker's server. Overflow in dest → RCE in minidlna (root).\n\nHmm wait — check the condition: `if (av_strncasecmp(&s[1617], \"WMServer/\", 9)) { if (s[568]==1) strcpy(dest, &s[1601]); }` — s[568]==1 means we did a second OPTIONS round... s[1601] is where the parsed \"Session:\"/\"Public:\"? Actually in ff_rtsp_parse_reply, s[1601] = ... this is the ffmpeg 1.x \"ff_rtsp_parse_reply\" storing the \"Public:\" header? In ffmpeg 0.x/1.x rtsp.c: replies have `rtsp->public_option` etc. Hard to be exact, but the strcpy(dest[64], response_field) with response-controlled content is real in this code shape.\n\nGiven time and the weak trigger (attacker must control an RTSP URL the router dereferences — typical for NAS media: file named \"rtsp://evil\" on USB; plausible but niche), this is a borderline finding. The scoring listed it as P1 6.05 with \"likely\" dataflow. Let me consider recording it with honest reachability caveats (static-only, trigger = media filename / RTSP URL pointing to attacker server; exploited post-scan). Confidence ~0.4.\n\nActually — wait, the more credible trigger: minidlna does NOT normally open \"rtsp://\" filenames — the DB scan uses real paths under /tmp/usbdisk. avformat_protocol detection: av_open_input checks the filename's protocol prefix. A file named \"rtsp://1.2.3.4/...\" on the filesystem would be opened with protocol rtsp if... actually av_open_input's `init_input` calls `av_probe_input_buffer` on the FILE — the file content would need to match RTSP demuxer. File content can't be forged easily. Hmm — actually ffmpeg's url_find_protocol matches the FILENAME prefix, so \"rtsp://...\" filename → uses rtsp protocol handler regardless of content? In ffmpeg, av_open_input: `av_find_input_format`/probe uses url protocol from filename if present (e.g. \"http://\" file is fetched via http). For rtsp:// filename with rtsp demuxer registered → connect. So a file NAMED \"rtsp://attacker:554/x\" on the NAS → minidlna thumbnail/metadata probe → RTSP connect → attacker response → the strcpy overflow. That's genuinely attacker-controllable IF attacker can create files on the NAS (SMB write access after admin auth... or physical USB). It's a post-config chain.\n\nI'll record this as static-only medium confidence 0.4 — it IS one of the top-scored static paths with a plausible trigger, and it's the remaining uncovered P1. Let me record it, then finalize the round with the (still not \"完成\") status.","stream":true,"block":0}

