r? In ffmpeg 0.x/1.x rtsp.c: replies have `rtsp->public_option` etc. Hard to be exact, but the strcpy(dest[64], response_field) with response-controlled content is real in this code shape.\n\nGiven time and the weak trigger (attacker must control an RTSP URL the router dereferences — typical for NAS media: file named \"rtsp://evil\" on USB; plausible but niche), this is a borderline finding. The scoring listed it as P1 6.05 with \"likely\" dataflow. Let me consider recording it with honest reachability caveats (static-only, trigger = media filename / RTSP URL pointing to attacker server; exploited post-scan). Confidence ~0.4.\n\nActually — wait, the more credible trigger: minidlna does NOT normally open \"rtsp://\" filenames — the DB scan uses real paths under /tmp/usbdisk. avformat_protocol detection: av_open_input checks the filename's protocol prefix. A file named \"rtsp://1.2.3.4/...\" on the filesystem would be opened with protocol rtsp if... actually av_open_input's `init_input` calls `av_probe_input_buffer` on the FILE — the file content would need to match RTSP demuxer. File content can't be forged easily. Hmm — actually ffmpeg's url_find_protocol matches the FILENAME prefix, so \"rtsp://...\" filename → uses rtsp protocol handler regardless of content? In ffmpeg, av_open_input: `av_find_input_format`/probe uses url protocol from filename if present (e.g. \"http://\" file is fetched via http). For rtsp:// filename with rtsp demuxer registered → connect. So a file NAMED \"rtsp://attacker:554/x\" on the NAS → minidlna thumbnail/metadata probe → RTSP connect → attacker response → the strcpy overflow. That's genuinely attacker-controllable IF attacker can create files on the NAS (SMB write access after admin auth... or physical USB). It's a post-config chain.\n\nI'll record this as static-only medium confidence 0.4 — it IS one of the top-scored static paths with a plausible trigger, and it's the remaining uncovered P1. Let me record it, then finalize the round with the (still not \"完成\") status.","stream":false,"block":0}

