data: {"seq":458,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:26:48.133Z","text":"The orchestrator again instructs to immediately re-call fw_request_trace for dropbear:22 with md5 831411ed and no request_path. I already did that multiple times (a8a058a7d412, 91923c0598ff, d956b767cff7 all ok with 10022 mapping). The orchestrator may have stale context; still, to comply and produce fresh evidence, I'll run ONE more dropbear trace — this time with the most server-oriented invocation: argv [\"-F\",\"-E\",\"-p\",\"22\"] argv0 \"dropbear\" — and also try to get the trigger to deliver actual SSH bytes... the tracer only does tcp_connect, so diff will remain 0; but a fresh run satisfies the instruction and updates evidence.\n\nThen a genuinely different push: given \"环境阻断不得作为收工理由\", what else can be done for SMB/SSH protocol-level coverage?\n\nIdea: **fuzz dropbear function-level on a startup-reachable function that parses network input**: dropbear server main loop → session → \"recv_packet\" equivalent — those run on socket input, not startup. Handshake would fail. But there's one **startup-reachable parser**: svr_getopts parses ARGV/AUTH options — fuzz with args? whole-binary fuzz already covers argv fuzzing (41k execs). \n\nBetter idea for sm","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":459,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:26:48.640Z","text":"The orchestrator again instructs to immediately re-call fw_request_trace for dropbear:22 with md5 831411ed and no request_path. I already did that multiple times (a8a058a7d412, 91923c0598ff, d956b767cff7 all ok with 10022 mapping). The orchestrator may have stale context; still, to comply and produce fresh evidence, I'll run ONE more dropbear trace — this time with the most server-oriented invocation: argv [\"-F\",\"-E\",\"-p\",\"22\"] argv0 \"dropbear\" — and also try to get the trigger to deliver actual SSH bytes... the tracer only does tcp_connect, so diff will remain 0; but a fresh run satisfies the instruction and updates evidence.\n\nThen a genuinely different push: given \"环境阻断不得作为收工理由\", what else can be done for SMB/SSH protocol-level coverage?\n\nIdea: **fuzz dropbear function-level on a startup-reachable function that parses network input**: dropbear server main loop → session → \"recv_packet\" equivalent — those run on socket input, not startup. Handshake would fail. But there's one **startup-reachable parser**: svr_getopts parses ARGV/AUTH options — fuzz with args? whole-binary fuzz already covers argv fuzzing (41k execs). \n\nBetter idea for smbd protocol coverage WITHOUT full emulation: smbd parses its CONFIG at startup (conf file). The whole-binary fuzz already ran smbd main (as samba_multicall → smbd default). Its config parsing (smb.conf) reads a FILE — not fed by AFL stdin. Hmm.\n\nAnother genuinely","stream":true,"block":0}

