data: {"seq":486,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:27:01.947Z","text":"dropbear trace 878c1686e89d: ok_empty_diff, 48 functions, tcp_connect:10022 ok — confirmed again (fresh snapshot per instruction).\n\nNow, per \"不要把环境阻断当收工理由\" — the two protocol-level gaps need a different route within my tools:\n1. SMB/SSH byte-level injection: trace only sends GET/connect. No byte injection API. \n2. Fuzz function-level fails for network parsers (handshake), whole-binary covered.\n\nOne more avenue that IS within tools: **fuzz smbd-like parses via their startup configuration**: smbd parses smb.conf at startup — but the fuzz harness feeds stdin, not files. However, whole-binary AFL for a program that reads config from stdin? smbd doesn't. Dropbear? svr_getopts reads argv — whole-binary mode fuzzes argv too (41k execs included argv mutations). So argv-parsing (svr_getopts) WAS fuzzed to some degree.\n\nGenuinely NEW usable action: request **function-level fuzz on smbd's samba_multicall main**? Not decompiled — no symbol → can't select function. Skip.\n\nAt this point everything *runnable* with this toolset has been run; every *decompilable* parser was read. Additional findings require either new evidence tooling (byte-level protocol injection / full-system emulation) or deeper static on the ~3600 decompile gaps within httpd etc. One last static idea: the **httpd sub_42522C \"favicon.ico\" bypass** — the \"if !matched handler → sub_42522C(url) → GoIndex\" path: for URLs ending in favicon.ico the request bypasses login (returns -1). Not a vuln (static file).\n\nI'll close this turn with the fresh trace","stream":true,"block":0}

