data: {"seq":601,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:31:00.889Z","text":"sub_40A714 (minidlna HTTP header parser) — reads all headers with scanner-style code (strchr-based, sets pointers straight into the receive buffer). All values stored as POINTERS into the request buffer (a1+56/60 SOAPAction len, a1+40 Content-Length atoi, Range strtoll, Callback ptr...) — no copies to fixed buffers → no overflow. Bounds: the outer loop `(unsigned int)s < a1+32 + a1+44` — within received data. Header loops read until \\r\\n or request end: e.g., the `while (*nptr < 48 || *nptr>=58) ++nptr` for Content-Length — runs past the buffer end if no digits?! `nptr = v36` (past ':') → scans forward until digit — if the header value has no digits and the buffer ends... the buffer is the request; the scan could run past `a1+32+a1+44` (received length) into adjacent heap (request struct is heap-allocated by Process_upnphttp via realloc'd","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":602,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:31:01.250Z","text":"sub_40A714 (minidlna HTTP header parser) — reads all headers with scanner-style code (strchr-based, sets pointers straight into the receive buffer). All values stored as POINTERS into the request buffer (a1+56/60 SOAPAction len, a1+40 Content-Length atoi, Range strtoll, Callback ptr...) — no copies to fixed buffers → no overflow. Bounds: the outer loop `(unsigned int)s < a1+32 + a1+44` — within received data. Header loops read until \\r\\n or request end: e.g., the `while (*nptr < 48 || *nptr>=58) ++nptr` for Content-Length — runs past the buffer end if no digits?! `nptr = v36` (past ':') → scans forward until digit — if the header value has no digits and the buffer ends... the buffer is the request; the scan could run past `a1+32+a1+44` (received length) into adjacent heap (request struct is heap-allocated by Process_upnphttp via realloc'd buffer + struct fields after). It reads bytes until a digit or beyond — the request buffer is null-terminated? Process_upnphttp sets `*(a1+32 + len)=0` after realloc (in state0). In state1 path the buffer may not be NUL-terminated... The scans could read a few bytes past into the STRUCT fields (a1+32 is the buffer start; the struct region after the buffer contains other fields — reads garbage, then atoi).","stream":true,"block":0}

