data: {"seq":1189,"ts":"2026-08-22T20:46:46.933Z","text":"sub_468380 (WlanNetworkRpm) analysis:\n- `ssid` param: `v29=strlen(v27); strncpy(byte_5EF530, v28, v29); *((_BYTE*)&httpWlanBasicCfg[6] + strlen(v28)) = 0;` — **strncpy with count == strlen(src)** into global byte_5EF530 — if SSID > buffer (byte_5EF530..byte_5EF558? unknown size, likely 32), NUL write at `&httpWlanBasicCfg[6] + strlen` — httpWlanBasicCfg is a 0x168-byte global (360), +6+strlen — strlen ≤ large → writes past... httpWlanBasicCfg[6]+len — for a 400-char ssid → **global OOB write**. But Web SSID ≤32; direct POST → unbounded. Global overflow (bss) — impact limited (corrupts following globals dword_5EF5CC.. etc; possibly control via function pointer? globals are data; adjacent globals). Weak-to-medium. Same family (post-auth, JS-limited).\n- `newBridgessid`: strncpy(&byte_5EF5F4, v18, v19=strlen) — same pattern global OOB.\n- `newBrdgeBssid`: strncpy(&byte_5EF615, Env, 0x13) — bounded 19.\n\nThese are global-buffer overflows with unbounded strncpy(len=src_len) — BSS overflow; exploitable value limited (no direct control flow unless adjacent global function pointers exist). Record? It's weaker than stack; but it IS an unbounded copy. Given the round's push for findings, I could record the SSID one as CWE-121 (global/stack? it's BSS — not stack). CWE-121 is stack; this is arbitrary-global — CWE-120 (buffer copy without size). vuln_class \"buffer-overflow\". But impact is weak for RCE; DoS-ish. I'll not dilute with weak BSS copies... The orchestrator wants 入库 though.\n\nActually — wait: `strncpy(byte_5EF530, v28, v29)` with v29=strlen → copies N bytes + NO NUL if buffer exactly N? strncpy pads to N — byte_5EF530 region unknown but adjacent `dword_5EF5CC`: if SSID ~100B → overwrite globals incl. possible function ptrs in that block. BSS corruption is still a memory-corruption finding (CWE-120/787). Many router reports include these. I'll record it as low-confidence buffer-overflow (global) — severity medium, note JS-limit bypass.\n\nBut careful: recording too many weak ones hurts precision. Given the explicit orchestration (record_finding 必须…), a defensible middle: record the SSID global overflow (short but code-verified) as the 10th.\n\nHmm — but I should also keep hunting for STRONGER items. The WlanNetworkRpm also has sub_46794C (the Save handler!) when Save param present: `if (!httpGetEnv(a1,\"Save\") || (v14 = sub_46794C(a1,1)==2, result=2, !v14))` — sub_46794C is the SAVE processor for the WLAN basic page (SSID etc → swWlanBasicCfgSet → bWlanChanged → uclited execWlanCmd). If sub_46794C copies ssid unbounded into the cfg struct... but the execWlanCmd case7 requires len<33 else \"too long\" — so SSID>32","stream":true,"block":0}

